var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Detection-and-Pose","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"This guide covers finding known boards in images with find_boards, which returns each board's homography or its pose relative to the camera. It also covers tracking boards through video with track_boards and setting up the GPU environment. The full keyword references are the find_boards and FindBoardsConfig docstrings; this guide does not repeat them.","title":"Detection and Pose"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#The-pipeline-in-one-call","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"find_boards takes three inputs:\n\na gallery of board descriptors. Each board's printed blobs are rendered, canonicalized and described once, then cached on disk (a BoardGallery).\na descriptor network (DescriptorNetwork): the ONNX model that describes both the gallery and the image.\nan image: grey, as an AbstractMatrix.\n\nFor each image it detects blobs, refines them to ellipses, describes them, and matches the descriptors against every board. For each candidate board it then fits the geometry with RANSAC and local optimization and gates the fit on inlier count and on the inliers' appearance. Each accepted board claims its image region, so no detection can serve two boards.","title":"The pipeline in one call"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Example:-find-the-boards-in-a-still","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"The example data ships through the package's artifact catalog: 50 board definitions (:boards), a still image with its camera calibration (:still) and a short clip (:video). Each downloads on first use. The descriptor network named by the default configuration (configs/default.toml) is fetched the same way, from the Hugging Face–hosted catalog entry.\n\nRun it from the checkout's gpu/ environment (julia --project=gpu, see The GPU environment), which provides every package it loads. On a machine without an NVIDIA GPU, leave out using CUDA, cuDNN and give the network execution_provider = :cpu; nothing else changes.\n\nusing CUDA, cuDNN                      # the GPU path (leave out for the CPU)\nusing BlobBoards\nusing CairoMakie                       # renders the example boards for the gallery (see below)\nusing FileIO, Colors                   # image loading\nimport VisualGeometryIo as VGIo\nusing VisualGeometryCore: CameraModel, is_map_inlier\n\nboards_dir = BlobBoards.example_data(:boards)\nstill = BlobBoards.example_data(:still)\n\n# the network named by configs/default.toml, fetched from the artifact catalog;\n# on the CPU: DescriptorNetwork(descriptor_network_path(); execution_provider = :cpu)\nnetwork = DescriptorNetwork(descriptor_network_path())\n\n# the gallery: board descriptors, cached on disk (the first build takes a while)\ngallery = BoardGallery(joinpath(tempdir(), \"example_gallery.jld2\"), network)\nboard_paths = filter(endswith(\".json\"), readdir(boards_dir; join = true))\nboard_features = Dict(insert!(gallery, board_paths, network))\nboard_bundles = Dict(b.uid => b.bundle for b in load_board.(board_paths))\n\nimg = Gray.(load(joinpath(still, \"DSC02847.JPG\")))\n\n# uncalibrated: one homography per board\nfound = find_boards(board_features, network, img; board_bundles)\nfor (uid, H, quality, scored) in found\n    println(uid, \": \", count(is_map_inlier, scored.weights), \" inliers\")\nend\n\nOn this still, four boards are found. The pipeline needs the board bundles as well as their features, because polarity, cutoff and each board's outline come from the bundle.\n\nThe example boards are definitions only: JSON, no PDF or PNG. Building the gallery needs each board's image, so it re-renders the stored board into a cache, and that render needs the authoring extension, using CairoMakie. Without it, insert! stops with an error that says so. Boards whose render sits beside their JSON need no CairoMakie.","title":"Example: find the boards in a still"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Calibrated-pose-or-homography","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"The camera lives in the geometry configuration, config.geometry. That one choice selects the estimator:\n\nconfig.geometry Needs model in each result\nHomographyGeometry() (default) nothing homography: board :world plane (mm, z = 0) → image pixels\nPoseGeometry(camera) a calibrated CameraModel board → camera pose: model.R, model.t (mm)\nRadialHomographyGeometry((width, height)) the image size homography plus a one-parameter division-model distortion, for an unknown camera whose lens bends lines\n\ncamera = VGIo.load(CameraModel, joinpath(still, \"cameras.json\"))\nconfig = FindBoardsConfig(geometry = PoseGeometry(camera))\nposed = find_boards(board_features, network, img; board_bundles, config)\n\nuid, pose, _, scored = first(posed)\npose.R, pose.t          # rotation and translation (mm), board world frame → camera frame\n\nThe pose path undistorts nothing by default. It detects on the raw image and verifies through the full camera model (PoseGeometry(camera; undistortion = :none)), so the inlier gate is in raw pixels everywhere on a strongly distorted lens.","title":"Calibrated pose or homography"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Reading-the-results","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"Each result is (uid, model, quality, scored):\n\nuid: the board's 3-character uid. It is printed in the board's corners and is a key of board_bundles. blobs(board_bundles[uid], :world) gives the board's points.\nmodel: the homography or pose (see the table above).\nquality: the fit's RANSAC score.\nscored: the board-blob → image-detection correspondences, with posterior weights in scored.weights. count(is_map_inlier, scored.weights) is the inlier count.","title":"Reading the results"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Checking-the-result-visually","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"Each board's model maps its :world plane into the image, so drawing the board through it shows at a glance whether the fit is right. board! draws a board's outline and blobs through any map, on any Makie backend:\n\nusing CairoMakie                      # or GLMakie, to open a window instead\nusing VisualGeometryCore: imshow, HomographyMap\nusing CoordinateTransformations: PerspectiveMap\nusing StaticArrays: SMatrix\nusing GeometryBasics: Rect\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1400, 950))\nax, _ = imshow(fig[1, 1], img)\nfor (uid, H, quality, scored) in found\n    bundle = board_bundles[uid]\n    proj = PerspectiveMap() ∘ HomographyMap(SMatrix{3, 3, Float64}(H))   # board world (mm) → pixels\n    board!(ax, proj, Rect(bundle, :world), blobs(bundle, :world); cutoff = cutoff(bundle))\nend\nCairoMakie.save(\"found_boards.png\", fig)   # with GLMakie: display(fig)\n\nexamples/pipeline/find_boards.jl does the same for both the homography and the pose path, with the inlier correspondences drawn. With a display it opens GLMakie windows; on a headless machine it saves the figures as PNGs.","title":"Checking the result visually"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Configuration","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"FindBoardsConfig holds every pipeline setting: detection (DetectConfig), adaptation, geometry, RANSAC bounds, the matching and appearance-verification thresholds, and the tracker. Its defaults come from the package's configs/default.toml. To record a run's configuration, keep it in a TOML file and load it with the camera as data:\n\nconfig = FindBoardsConfig(\"configs/default.toml\")                      # homography\nconfig = FindBoardsConfig(\"my_run.toml\"; camera)                       # [find_boards.geometry] type = \"pose\"\n\nTo change one setting, rebuild the immutable config with Accessors.@set, as shown in the FindBoardsConfig docstring.","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Video:-track_boards","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"For video, track_boards carries each board from frame to frame: motion-guided association, then a per-frame fit over the associated blobs. Full discovery (the find_boards path) runs only on keyframes. It takes a video path or any sequence of grey frames:\n\nvideo = BlobBoards.example_data(:video)\nclip = joinpath(video, \"blobboards_handheld.mov\")\ntracks = track_boards(board_features, network, clip; board_bundles)\n# Dict(uid => Vector{BoardDetection}), one detection per frame the board was found in\n\nUnrelated stills belong in find_boards with a vector of frames instead. That runs each frame independently and still stages the gallery only once.","title":"Video: track_boards"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#gpu-env","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"Every stage of the pipeline is written against KernelAbstractions. The device keyword picks the backend: KA.CPU() runs the threaded host code, and CUDABackend() runs the device kernels. The default, default_device(), picks the GPU when CUDA is loaded and functional, and the CPU otherwise. The descriptor network runs in ONNX Runtime, on its CUDA execution provider or its CPU one.\n\nCUDA and cuDNN are weak dependencies of BlobBoards. As with CairoMakie for authoring, a package's own project cannot load its weak dependencies. The checkout ships a companion environment, gpu/, that dev's the checkout and adds:\n\nPackage Activates\nCUDA BlobBoardsCUDAExt (the device kernels; default_device() becomes the GPU)\ncuDNN ONNX Runtime's CUDA execution provider (needs CUDA and cuDNN loaded)\nCUDARuntimejll, pinned to 12.9 ONNX Runtime's CUDA provider requires a 12.x runtime\nGLMakie interactive inspection: overlay windows (examples/pipeline/find_boards.jl)\nFileIO, ImageIO, Colors, CairoMakie, GeometryBasics, … what the example and training scripts load\n\njulia --project=gpu -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'   # once\njulia --project=gpu -t auto                             # GPU REPL\n\nOn a machine without a display (a compute node, SSH without X forwarding), GLMakie cannot precompile, because its OpenGL setup needs a display, and Pkg.instantiate reports it as one errored dependency. That is harmless: nothing that runs headless loads GLMakie.\n\nThe pixi environment adds only Ghostscript (for authoring) and the Python interface; CUDA comes from Julia's own artifacts, so use pixi run julia --project=gpu only if you also want those.","title":"The GPU environment"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Requirements","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"An NVIDIA GPU of compute capability 7.5 (Turing) or newer, the minimum of CUDA.jl 5.11, the version gpu/ resolves to.\nAn NVIDIA driver for CUDA 12 or newer. CUDA.jl requires it, and ONNX Runtime's CUDA provider (ONNX Runtime 1.20.1) needs a 12.x CUDA runtime, hence the 12.9 pin below. On some systems CUDA.jl can load a newer driver library for the session (CUDA forward compatibility); CUDA.versioninfo() says when it does.\nLinux or Windows on x86-64. These are the platforms CUDA.jl supports and for which ONNX Runtime.jl ships a GPU build. macOS has no CUDA: there the pipeline runs on the CPU only.\nNo CUDA toolkit to install. CUDA.jl downloads the CUDA runtime, cuBLAS and cuDNN as Julia artifacts, and ONNXRunTime.jl its own GPU build of ONNX Runtime, so only the driver must be on the system.\nGPU memory: about 6 GiB for a 12 MP image (gpu/ was checked on a 180 GiB B200, where more fit). The descriptor network's ONNX Runtime arena takes up to 2 GiB (DescriptorNetwork's default max_batch = 64). Detection keeps one image pyramid per tilt of the affine-simulation schedule on the GPU, about 2.8 GiB each at 12 MP, so it needs at least one, and it holds as many of the 25 as fit in 80% of the free memory. A bigger card is therefore faster, not required. The pyramids scale with the image's pixel count.\nMeasured on that B200, for the example still (12 MP, 50 boards), after warm-up: find_boards took 1.3 s per image on the GPU against 16 s on the CPU (224 threads). Both found the same four boards, with poses agreeing to 3·10⁻⁵ mm.","title":"Requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Without-a-checkout","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"A registry install (Pkg.add(\"BlobBoards\")) has no gpu/ environment. Make your own project with the triggers and the runtime pin. The pin must be in your project, either as a [preferences] table in its Project.toml or as a LocalPreferences.toml beside it:\n\n# Project.toml of your environment\n[deps]\nBlobBoards = \"93a5632c-6c4c-4c98-b3b2-221d1d9ee9e0\"\nCUDA = \"052768ef-5323-5732-b1bb-66c8b64840ba\"\nCUDA_Runtime_jll = \"76a88914-d11a-5bdc-97e0-2f5a05c973a2\"\ncuDNN = \"02a925ec-e4fe-4b08-9a7e-0d78e3d38ccd\"\n\n[preferences.CUDA_Runtime_jll]\nversion = \"12.9\"\n\nOr interactively:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add([\"BlobBoards\", \"CUDA\", \"cuDNN\", \"CUDA_Runtime_jll\"])\nusing CUDA\nCUDA.set_runtime_version!(v\"12.9\")   # writes the pin to LocalPreferences.toml; restart Julia\n\nAfter the restart, the check below must report :cuda. Add CairoMakie for the example boards (definitions only), and FileIO, Colors and ImageIO to load images.\n\nThe pin matters because the newest CUDA runtime CUDA.jl would otherwise pick can be 13.x, and ONNX Runtime 1.20's CUDA provider accepts only 12.x, so DescriptorNetwork would refuse to start on the GPU.","title":"Without a checkout"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#From-Python","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"The blobboards Python package runs Julia in the checkout's authoring/ environment, which lists CUDA, cuDNN and the pinned CUDA 12.9 runtime. Its find_boards runs on the GPU when one is usable (device=\"auto\", the default) and on the CPU otherwise, with a warning; each result records its device and execution_provider, and blobboards.device() reports the choice up front. See GPU or CPU in the Python guide.","title":"From Python"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Making-sure-the-GPU-is-used","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"Two things must both be true. Check them before trusting a timing:\n\nusing BlobBoards, CUDA, cuDNN\nCUDA.functional()                     # true: a usable GPU and driver\nBlobBoards.default_device()           # CUDABackend(): the kernels run on the GPU\nnetwork = DescriptorNetwork(descriptor_network_path())\nnetwork.session.execution_provider    # :cuda: the network runs on the GPU\n\nBy default DescriptorNetwork throws if the CUDA execution provider cannot start. The CPU provider is about 34× slower on this network, so the constructor refuses rather than report a misleading speed. The usual cause is using CUDA without cuDNN; a full card at construction is another. To choose the CPU deliberately, use DescriptorNetwork(path; execution_provider = :cpu). allow_cpu_fallback = true restores the old behaviour: warn and continue on the CPU. With that option, network.session.execution_provider is the only reliable way to see which provider is in use.","title":"Making sure the GPU is used"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#CPU-only","page":"Detection and Pose","text":"Without CUDA loaded, default_device() is the CPU and every stage runs there; only the network needs execution_provider = :cpu, as in the example above. It needs no gpu/ environment, just patience. On the example still (12 MP, 50 boards), the CPU path took about 3 minutes to build the gallery once and 16 s per image after warm-up (224 threads).","title":"CPU only"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Coordinate-Systems","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"BlobBoards uses three types of coordinate specifications. Understanding them is essential for correctly extracting and using blob positions.\n\nType Values Purpose\nImage Origin :colmap, :opencv, :julia Pixel coordinate convention\nReference Frame :pattern, :board, :canvas Spatial origin on boards\nCoordinate System :logical, :physical, :world Unit system for transforms","title":"Coordinate Systems"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Image-Origins","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"Different software uses different conventions for where the \"first pixel\" is located. BlobBoards stores coordinates internally in :colmap convention.\n\nOrigin First Pixel Center Common Usage\n:colmap (0.5, 0.5) COLMAP, SfM tools, BlobBoards internal\n:opencv (0, 0) OpenCV, Python, NumPy\n:julia (1, 1) Julia arrays, MATLAB","title":"Image Origins"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Visual-Representation","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"     :colmap               :opencv               :julia\n  corner at (0,0)       corner at (0,0)      corner at (0.5,0.5)\n        ↓                     ↓                     ↓\n   ┌─────────┐          ┌─────────┐          ┌─────────┐\n   │(0.5,0.5)│          │  (0,0)  │          │  (1,1)  │\n   │    •    │          │    •    │          │    •    │\n   │  pixel  │          │  pixel  │          │  pixel  │\n   └─────────┘          └─────────┘          └─────────┘","title":"Visual Representation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Converting-Between-Image-Origins","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"using BlobBoards\nusing CoordinateTransformations: Translation\n\n# Image-origin conversion is a Translation — composable, callable on points/Discs/blobs\nT = Translation(:colmap, :opencv)            # Translation(Vec2(-0.5, -0.5) * px)\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern)                  # always in :colmap convention\nb_opencv = T.(b)                             # shift every blob in place\n\nT = Translation(:colmap, :julia)\njulia_point = T(colmap_point)\n\n# Strip units when composing with plain-Float64 maps\nunitless = ustrip(T)","title":"Converting Between Image Origins"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Reference-Frames","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"For calibration boards, three nested reference frames define where the coordinate origin is located.\n\nFrame Origin Location Use Case\n:pattern Top-left of pattern area Blob detection, feature matching\n:board Top-left of board (includes frame) Full board image processing\n:canvas Top-left of page (includes margins) Page layout, printing","title":"Reference Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Layout-Diagram","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"    ◀──────────────────── canvas_size.width ────────────────────▶\n\n    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   ▲\n    │ ○ :canvas origin                                          │   │\n    │  (0,0)                                                    │   │\n    │    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │   │\n    │    │ ○ :board origin                                │     │   │\n    │    │  (0,0)                                         │     │   │\n    │    │    ┌────────────────────────────────────┐      │     │   │\n    │    │    │ ○ :pattern origin                  │      │     │   │\n    │    │    │  (0,0)                             │      │     │   │\n    │    │    │                                    │      │     │   │\n    │◀─▶│◀──▶│        ┌───┐                       │◀────▶│◀───▶│   │\n    │ m │ f  │        │ ● │  blob                 │  f   │  m  │   c\n    │ a │ r  │        └───┘                       │  r   │  a  │   a\n    │ r │ a  │                                    │  a   │  r  │   n\n    │ g │ m  │                                    │  m   │  g  │   v\n    │ i │ e  │                                    │  e   │  i  │   a\n    │ n │    │        pattern_size                │      │  n  │   s\n    │   │    │                                    │      │     │   _\n    │   │    │                                    │      │     │   s\n    │   │    └────────────────────────────────────┘      │     │   i\n    │   │    ◀────── pattern_size.width ─────────▶       │     │   z\n    │   │                                                │     │   e\n    │   └────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │   .\n    │   ◀─────────────  board_size.width  ──────────────▶      │   h\n    │                                                          │   e\n    └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   i\n                                                                   g\n    Legend:                                                        h\n    ○ = coordinate origin (top-left corner)                        t\n    ● = blob center                                                ▼\n    m = margin (canvas edge to board edge)\n    f = frame_width (board edge to pattern edge)","title":"Layout Diagram"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Converting-Between-Reference-Frames","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"bundle, _ = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Get Translation between frames\nT = Translation(board_config(bundle), :pattern, :board)\nboard_point = T(pattern_point)\n\n# Or extract blobs directly in desired frame\nb_pattern = blobs(bundle, :pattern)\nb_canvas = blobs(bundle, :canvas)","title":"Converting Between Reference Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Coordinate-Values-Example","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"For a blob at pattern coordinates (80px, 120px) with frame_width=75px:\n\nFrame Blob Center\n:pattern (80px, 120px)\n:board (155px, 195px)\n:canvas (155px, 195px) (same if no margin)","title":"Coordinate Values Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Coordinate-Systems-2","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"For transforms between pixel and physical units, use :logical, :physical, and :world:\n\nSystem Units Description\n:logical px Pixel coordinates (requires image_origin)\n:physical mm Physical coordinates in pattern frame\n:world unitless mm Centered origin, y-axis up","title":"Coordinate Systems"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Logical-to-Physical-Transform","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"bundle, _ = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Get affine transform from pixels to mm\nH = AffineMap(bundle, :logical, :physical; image_origin=:julia)\n\n# Transform a pixel point to physical coordinates\nphysical_pt = H(Point2(100.0, 200.0))","title":"Logical to Physical Transform"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Logical-to-World-Transform","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"# Direct transform from pixels to world frame\nH = AffineMap(bundle, :logical, :world; image_origin=:opencv)\nworld_pt = H(Point2(100.0, 200.0))","title":"Logical to World Transform"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Blob-Extraction","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"","title":"Blob Extraction"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Unified-API","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"The blobs function takes a reference frame (positional) and optional units:\n\nbundle, _ = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Pixel units (default) in pattern frame\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern)\n\n# Pixel units in canvas frame\nb = blobs(bundle, :canvas)\n\n# Physical units\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern; units=mm)\nb = blobs(bundle, :canvas; units=inch)","title":"Unified API"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Saddle-Points","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"For boards with checkerboard frames (:saddle style):\n\n# Pixel units\nsp = saddle_points(bundle, :pattern)\n\n# Physical units\nsp = saddle_points(bundle, :pattern; units=mm)","title":"Saddle Points"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#World-Coordinates","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"For 3D calibration (centered origin, y-up, mm):\n\nb = blobs(bundle, :world)\n# origin at pattern center\n# x-axis right, y-axis up\n# units: mm (stripped to Float64)\n\n# Lift to 3D points on z=0 plane\nusing GeometryBasics: Point3\nX = Point3.(vcat.(centroid.(b), 0.0))","title":"World Coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Bounding-Rectangles","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"Get rectangles for different regions:\n\nusing GeometryBasics: Rect\n\nbundle, _ = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Pattern bounds (origin at 0,0)\nrect = Rect(bundle, :pattern)\n\n# Board bounds (extends into negative in pattern coords)\nrect = Rect(bundle, :board)\n# rect.origin = (-frame_width, -frame_width) in pattern frame","title":"Bounding Rectangles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Common-Workflows","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"","title":"Common Workflows"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Feature-Detection-Pipeline","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"bundle, image = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Detect blobs (your detector returns OpenCV convention)\ndetected = detect_blobs(image)\n\n# Get ground truth, convert to OpenCV convention\ngt = blobs(bundle, :canvas)\ngt_opencv = Translation(:colmap, :opencv).(gt)\n\n# Match and evaluate\nmatches = match_blobs(detected, gt_opencv)","title":"Feature Detection Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Camera-Calibration","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"bundle, image = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Get 3D points (world frame, z=0)\nb = blobs(bundle, :world)\nX = Point3.(vcat.(centroid.(b), 0.0))\n\n# Get 2D image points (from detection)\nx = detect_and_match(image, b)\n\n# Estimate camera pose\ncamera = estimate_pose(X, x)","title":"Camera Calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Unit-Conversions","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"Convert between physical and logical units:\n\nusing BlobBoards                     # re-exports mm, px, dpi\nusing VisualGeometryCore: uconvert\nusing GeometryBasics: Point2\n\nbundle, _ = BlobBoards.load(\"blob_board.json\")\nconfig = board_config(bundle)\nprint_density = config.print_density  # e.g., 300 dpi\n\n# Use helper functions\nscale = px_per_mm(print_density)  # pixels per mm\nscale = mm_per_px(print_density)  # mm per pixel (with units)\n\n# Physical to logical\nphysical_point = Point2(10.0mm, 20.0mm)\nlogical_point = uconvert.(px, physical_point .* print_density)\n\n# Logical to physical\nlogical_point = Point2(100.0px, 200.0px)\nphysical_point = uconvert.(mm, logical_point ./ print_density)","title":"Unit Conversions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coordinates/#Summary-Table","page":"Coordinate Systems","text":"What Keyword/Argument Values Default\nPixel convention image_origin (keyword) :colmap, :opencv, :julia :colmap (internal)\nSpatial origin reference frame (positional) :pattern, :board, :canvas required\nUnit system units (keyword) px, mm, inch, etc. px","title":"Summary Table"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Authoring-a-Board","page":"Boards","text":"This guide is about making a printable calibration board with blob_board: setting up the environment, worked examples, output formats, printing and reproducing a board later. Every method form and every keyword (defaults, units, valid values) is listed once, in the blob_board reference. This guide shows them in use but does not repeat that list.","title":"Authoring a Board"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#The-authoring-environment","page":"Boards","text":"Board authoring (figure layout and PDF/TIFF/PNG export) lives in the BlobBoardsAuthoringExt package extension. Julia loads it when CairoMakie is loaded. CairoMakie is a weak dependency of BlobBoards, so using BlobBoards (detection, patterns) never pays for Makie. The catch is that Julia never loads a package's own weak dependencies from that package's project, so julia --project=. in a checkout cannot load CairoMakie. Without the extension, blob_board is a stub with no methods:\n\njulia> blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi)\nERROR: MethodError: no method matching blob_board(::String, ::DynamicQuantities.Quantity{…})\nThe function `blob_board` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types.\n\nThe checkout ships a companion environment, authoring/, for this. It dev's the checkout and adds, as ordinary dependencies:\n\nPackage Activates For\nCairoMakie BlobBoardsAuthoringExt, BlobBoardsMakieExt blob_board, render_board, PDF/PNG export, the inspection plots\nLibtiff_jll BlobBoardsTiffExt format = \"tiff\" (the LZW TIFF writer)\nPythonCall BlobBoardsPythonExt the Python package (python/blobboards)\n\njulia --project=authoring -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'   # once\njulia --project=authoring                                     # authoring REPL\njulia --project=authoring examples/generate/board.jl          # A4, 300 dpi, PDF\n\nPDF output goes through Ghostscript, and PDF/X-4 (the default) needs Ghostscript ≥ 10.07. The repository's pixi environment provides it, so with pixi installed run Julia through it:\n\npixi install\npixi run julia --project=authoring\n\nIf you installed BlobBoards from a registry instead of a checkout, add the triggers to your own environment (Pkg.add([\"BlobBoards\", \"CairoMakie\"]), plus Libtiff_jll for TIFF). Patterns (blob_pattern) need only the core package.","title":"The authoring environment"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Your-first-board","page":"Boards","text":"using BlobBoards      # re-exports the units: mm, inch, px, dpi, …\nusing CairoMakie      # loads the board-authoring extension\n\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; seed = 0xBEEF, dir = \"./output\")\n\nThis writes two files named after the board's uid:\n\nblob_board_<uid>.pdf: the print master, PDF/X-4.\nblob_board_<uid>.json: the sidecar. It records the configuration and every blob, which is enough to reproduce or re-render the board and to detect it.\n\nThe returned BoardBundle holds the same data, so with dir left out nothing is written and you get the bundle in memory. seed makes the placement reproducible; leave it out and a random seed is drawn and recorded.","title":"Your first board"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Page-and-board-size","page":"Boards","text":"The page (canvas) can be a paper name, a width and height, or a Size2. The board can be smaller than the page; it is then centred, with white margins:\n\nbundle = blob_board(\"Letter\", 300dpi; dir = \"./output\")          # a paper name (BlobBoards.PRINT_SIZES)\nbundle = blob_board(200mm, 280mm, 300dpi; dir = \"./output\")      # width × height\nbundle = blob_board(8inch, 10inch, 600dpi; dir = \"./output\")     # inches work too\nbundle = blob_board(Size2(width = 200mm, height = 280mm), 300dpi; dir = \"./output\")\n\n# a 180 mm square board centred on an A4 page\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi;\n    board_size = Size2(width = 180mm, height = 180mm), dir = \"./output\")\n\nThe layout is three nested rectangles: the canvas (the page), the board (board_size, ≤ the canvas, default the whole page) and the pattern (the board minus a frame_width band on each side).","title":"Page and board size"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Blobs","page":"Boards","text":"blob_sizes = (min, max) are the smallest and largest printed disc diameters, as physical lengths converted to printer dots through the print density:\n\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi;\n    blob_sizes = (4.0mm, nothing),   # (min, max); nothing = largest disc is 20% of the short side\n    seed = 0x1234,\n    dir = \"./output\")\n\n# printed discs from 2 mm to 12 mm across\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; blob_sizes = (2mm, 12mm), dir = \"./output\")\n\n# the max as a fraction of the pattern's short side\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; blob_sizes = (4.0mm, 0.2short_side), dir = \"./output\")\n\n# light blobs on a black background\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; polarity = :light, dir = \"./output\")\n\nThe default min is 2·cutoff·1mm (4 mm at the default cutoff). The smallest disc must be at least 2·cutoff·2 printer dots across (σ ≥ 2 dots): 8 dots at the default cutoff. In millimetres:\n\nPrint density Smallest disc diameter\n150 dpi 1.35 mm\n300 dpi 0.68 mm\n600 dpi 0.34 mm\n1200 dpi 0.17 mm\n\nA smaller min is an ArgumentError that states this floor in mm at your density. The placement keywords (scales_per_octave, α, cutoff, max_trials) are described in the reference and the Patterns guide.","title":"Blobs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Frame-and-ruler","page":"Boards","text":"┌─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  QR  │    North Ruler    │  UID[0] │\n├──────┼───────────────────┼─────────┤\n│      │                   │         │\n│ West │      Pattern      │  East   │\n│Ruler │       Area        │  Ruler  │\n│      │                   │         │\n├──────┼───────────────────┼─────────┤\n│UID[1]│    South Ruler    │  UID[2] │\n└─────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nThe frame is a band of frame_width around the pattern (default 7.5% of the page's longer side). Its corners hold the QR code, which encodes the board's configuration, and the three uid characters. If the QR code does not fit its corner at this density, it is omitted with a warning. frame_width = 0mm gives a frameless board: no rulers, QR code or uid.\n\n# ruler frame (the default), wider band, ticks every 10 mm / 5 mm\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; frame_style = :ruler, frame_width = 15mm,\n    major_tick_spacing = 10mm, minor_tick_spacing = 5mm, dir = \"./output\")\n\n# checkerboard frame: the bundle also carries its saddle points\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; frame_style = :saddle, frame_padding = 2mm, dir = \"./output\")\n\n# blank frame: corners (QR, uid) only\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; frame_style = :none, dir = \"./output\")\n\nframe_padding adds white space between the frame's content (ticks, saddle squares, QR code, uid glyphs) and the band's edges.","title":"Frame and ruler"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Ruler-widths","page":"Boards","text":"major_tick_width and minor_tick_width are each tick's full drawn length across the band, centred in it. By default the major ticks fill the padded band (major_tick_width = frame_width - 2frame_padding) and the minor ticks are half as long. Widths must satisfy 0 < minor_tick_width ≤ major_tick_width ≤ frame_width - 2frame_padding; anything else is an ArgumentError.\n\n# 15 mm band; major ticks 8 mm long, minor ticks 4 mm, centred in the band\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; frame_width = 15mm,\n    major_tick_width = 8mm, minor_tick_width = 4mm, dir = \"./output\")","title":"Ruler widths"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Output-formats","page":"Boards","text":"format is exactly \"pdf\", \"tiff\" (or \"tif\") or \"png\". format, pdf_standard and icc_profile describe the file written to dir, so they are checked before placement. Each of these is an ArgumentError:\n\nany of them without a dir\npdf_standard with a raster format\nicc_profile with a PNG","title":"Output formats"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#PDF-(default)","page":"Boards","text":"bundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; dir = \"./output\")                       # PDF/X-4\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; pdf_standard = :x1a, dir = \"./output\")  # PDF 1.3, older RIPs\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; pdf_standard = :none, dir = \"./output\") # plain PDF, no OutputIntent\n\nPDF/X-4 by default (pdf_standard = :x4, what most print shops ask for); :x3 or :x1a for older RIPs, :none for a plain PDF\nan OutputIntent declaring the print condition (linear-reflectance grey by default; see ICC profiles below) and a TrimBox at the board area\nthe pattern embedded pixel for pixel at the print density: lossless Flate, no downsampling, and no interpolation flag (a print master is not to be smoothed)\nvector rulers and text\n\nAn older Ghostscript is refused with an error rather than allowed to write a non-conformant file.","title":"PDF (default)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#TIFF","page":"Boards","text":"For print shops and photo labs that take raster files (needs using Libtiff_jll, which the authoring/ environment provides):\n\nusing Libtiff_jll\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 1200dpi; format = \"tiff\", dir = \"./output\")\n\nthe whole sheet at exactly the print density, 8-bit grey, with the pattern pixels identical to the PDF's\nthe density in the resolution tags, so the print software sizes it exactly\nthe ICC profile embedded (linear reflectance by default)\nLZW compression with the horizontal predictor","title":"TIFF"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#PNG","page":"Boards","text":"For digital use (rendering, training):\n\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; format = \"png\", dir = \"./output\")\n\nThe PNG is single-channel grey with linear gamma (file_gamma = 1.0).","title":"PNG"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#ICC-profiles","page":"Boards","text":"A PDF/X file's OutputIntent says how its grey values are to be printed. The default, :linear, declares them linear reflectance: a value of 0.5 reflects half the light of bare paper, so a Gaussian in the file is a Gaussian in the light the camera sees. A colour-managed RIP (print shops) honours it; an office printer ignores it and applies its own tone curve.\n\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; icc_profile = :linear, dir = \"./output\")       # the default\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; icc_profile = \"/path/to/press_gray.icc\", dir = \"./output\")","title":"ICC profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Identity-Label","page":"Boards","text":"Every new board prints its identity in one 6 pt line under the board, in the sheet margin (outside the TrimBox, clear of the printer's unprintable border):\n\nblob_board_e98_fdac4 · sha256 e98fdac4… (64 hex) · BlobBoards 0.36.0 · 200 × 200 mm @ 1200 dpi\n\nso every printed sheet — frameless boards included — can be traced to its JSON. It is a rendering option, not part of the board's identity: id_label = false turns it off without changing the UID. A board that fills its sheet has no room for it, and the label is skipped (with a warning for a frameless board, which would otherwise carry no printed identity). The JSON's render block records whether it was drawn: blob_board(json) and the gallery's regeneration rebuild the sheet as recorded, and a board with no render block (written before 0.36.2) rebuilds without one. render_board defaults to false.","title":"Identity Label"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Printing","page":"Boards","text":"A board is a measuring instrument, so it must be printed at exactly 100%.\n\nIn the print dialog choose \"Actual size\" / 100%. Turn off \"Fit\", \"Shrink oversized pages\" and \"Scale to printable area\". An A4 page is larger than any printer's printable area, so a dialog left on \"Fit\" shrinks the whole board by about 3%. PDF/X-4 output asks Acrobat/Reader for actual size (/PrintScaling /None); other viewers may ignore it.\nIf your printer cannot print to the page edge and keeps scaling, make the board smaller than the page: board_size leaves a white margin, so the page prints at 100% without anything being clipped.\nPrint grey / black ink only, at the highest quality, with image enhancement off.\nCheck every print. Measure the rulers: the major ticks are major_tick_spacing apart, so at the default spacing ten intervals must span exactly 100.0 mm. On a frameless board, measure the pattern edge to edge. A \"fit\" print comes out about 3% short.\nPrint shops print PDF/X at 100% by default; the TrimBox marks the board.","title":"Printing"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Reproducing-a-board","page":"Boards","text":"The JSON sidecar is the board's definition. There are three ways to use it.\n\n# 1. Load the bundle (and its image): the input to detection and coordinates\nbundle, image = BlobBoards.load(\"./output/blob_board_<uid>.json\")\n\n# 2. Re-render the stored board (its recorded blobs, frame, QR and uid);\n#    no placement is re-run, so it matches the printed board exactly\nBlobBoards.render_board(bundle; dir = \"./rerender\", format = \"png\")\n\n# 3. Re-generate from the configuration (placement is re-run from the seed)\nbundle = blob_board(read(\"./output/blob_board_<uid>.json\", String); dir = \"./output2\")","title":"Reproducing a board"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Reading-a-board-back","page":"Boards","text":"bundle, image = BlobBoards.load(\"./output/blob_board_<uid>.json\")\n\nblobs(bundle, :pattern)              # px, pattern frame (COLMAP convention)\nblobs(bundle, :board; units = mm)    # mm, board frame (includes the frame band)\nblobs(bundle, :world)                # centred, y-up, unitless mm: the z = 0 calibration plane\n\nboard_config(bundle).print_density   # the BoardConfig\npattern_config(bundle).seed          # the PatternConfig\n\nSee Coordinate Systems for the reference frames and transforms.","title":"Reading a board back"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/boards/#Full-keyword-reference","page":"Boards","text":"All method forms and keywords, with defaults and valid values, are in the blob_board docstring. Paper names are the keys of BlobBoards.PRINT_SIZES.","title":"Full keyword reference"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Quick-Reference","page":"Quick Reference","text":"This page provides a comprehensive overview of the main entry points and all their parameters.","title":"Quick Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Entry-Points","page":"Quick Reference","text":"BlobBoards has two main entry points:\n\nFunction Purpose Output\nblob_pattern Generate blob patterns for training/simulation (pattern, blobs, config)\nblob_board Generate printable calibration boards BoardBundle","title":"Entry Points"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#blob_pattern-Pattern-Generation","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Generate multi-scale blob patterns in pixel/logical units.","title":"blob_pattern - Pattern Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Signatures","page":"Quick Reference","text":"# From dimensions\nblob_pattern(width, height; kwargs...)\nblob_pattern(Size2(width=W, height=H); kwargs...)\n\n# With explicit pixel type\nblob_pattern(Gray{N0f8}, Size2(width=W, height=H); kwargs...)\n\n# From config object\nblob_pattern(config::PatternConfig; dir=nothing)","title":"Signatures"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#All-Keyword-Arguments","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Parameter Type Default Description\nblob_sizes Tuple (8px, nothing) (min, max) disc diameters (2·cutoff·σ) in logical units (px; a bare number is px). max = nothing → largest disc = 20% of the short side; 0.2short_side → a fraction of the short side. Default min = 2·cutoff·2px, also the floor\nseed Integer random Random seed for reproducibility (UInt64)\nscales_per_octave Int 3 Number of scales per doubling of size\nα Float64 1.2 Scale sampling bias (higher = more small blobs)\ncutoff Float64 2.0 Gaussian truncation radius in σ — use the default 2\nmax_trials Int 5000 Max placement attempts per blob\nraster_aligned Bool true Snap blob centers to pixel centers\npolarity Symbol :dark Blob polarity (:dark or :light)\ndir String nothing Output directory (saves PNG + JSON if provided)","title":"All Keyword Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Stored-Configuration-(PatternConfig)","page":"Quick Reference","text":"The returned PatternConfig (and the pattern_config block of a v2 JSON file) stores the blob sizes as σ in logical units (a disc's diameter is 2·cutoff·σ):\n\nField Type Description\nmin_scale LogicalCount Smallest σ (px) — the bottom rung of the scale ladder\nmax_scale LogicalCount Largest σ (px) — the top of the scale ladder, exactly as requested\n\nPatternConfig(; pattern_resolution, min_scale, max_scale, …) takes these storage-level σ values directly (max_scale = nothing applies the default rule).","title":"Stored Configuration (PatternConfig)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Examples","page":"Quick Reference","text":"using BlobBoards\nusing VisualGeometryCore: px\n\n# Basic usage\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), seed=0x1234)\n\n# Denser scale sampling\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600;\n    blob_sizes = (12px, nothing),\n    scales_per_octave = 4,\n    seed = 0x1234\n)\n\n# Save to disk\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600;\n    blob_sizes = (12px, nothing),\n    dir = \"./output\"\n)","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#blob_board-Board-Generation","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Generate printable calibration boards with borders, rulers, and metadata.","title":"blob_board - Board Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Signatures-2","page":"Quick Reference","text":"# From paper size name\nblob_board(paper_size::String, print_density; kwargs...)\n\n# From explicit dimensions\nblob_board(width, height, print_density; kwargs...)\nblob_board(Size2(width=W, height=H), print_density; kwargs...)\n\n# From JSON (reconstruction, as printed: format / pdf_standard / id_label\n# default to the JSON's `render` block; a keyword overrides it)\nblob_board(json_string; dir=nothing, format=nothing, pdf_standard=nothing, id_label=nothing)","title":"Signatures"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Keyword-Arguments","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Every keyword — output (dir, format, pdf_standard, icc_profile, id_label), pattern (blob_sizes, seed, polarity, …), board and frame (board_size, frame_width, frame_style, frame_padding) and ruler ticks — with its default and valid values is listed in the blob_board reference.","title":"Keyword Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Examples-2","page":"Quick Reference","text":"using BlobBoards      # re-exports the units: mm, inch, px, dpi, …\nusing CairoMakie      # loads the board-authoring extension\n\n# Basic A4 board\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; dir=\"./output\")\n\n# Custom size with parameters\nbundle = blob_board(200mm, 280mm, 300dpi;\n    blob_sizes = (3.2mm, nothing),\n    frame_width = 25mm,\n    seed = 0x1234,\n    dir = \"./output\"\n)\n\n# Saddle frame with padding\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi;\n    blob_sizes = (4.0mm, nothing),\n    frame_style = :saddle,\n    frame_padding = 2mm,\n    dir = \"./output\"\n)\n\n# Blank frame (corners only)\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi;\n    blob_sizes = (4.0mm, nothing),\n    frame_style = :none,\n    dir = \"./output\"\n)\n\n# High-resolution for printing\nbundle = blob_board(\"A3\", 600dpi;\n    blob_sizes = (2.0mm, nothing),\n    format = \"pdf\",\n    icc_profile = :linear,\n    dir = \"./output\"\n)\n\n# PNG for digital use\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi;\n    format = \"png\",\n    dir = \"./output\"\n)","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Available-Paper-Sizes","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Category Sizes\nISO A-series A0, A1, A2, A3, A4, A5\nANSI ANSI A, ANSI B, ANSI C, ANSI D, ANSI E, ANSI E1\nCommon Letter, US Letter, Legal, Tabloid, Ledger","title":"Available Paper Sizes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Blob-and-Saddle-Point-Extraction","page":"Quick Reference","text":"","title":"Blob and Saddle Point Extraction"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#blobs-Unified-Extraction","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Extract blobs with specified reference frame and units:\n\nbundle, image = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Pixel units (default) in pattern frame\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern)\n\n# Physical units\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern; units=mm)\nb = blobs(bundle, :canvas; units=inch)\n\n# From PatternBundle (no reference frame needed)\nb = blobs(pattern_bundle)","title":"blobs - Unified Extraction"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#saddle_points-(Boards-with-:saddle-frame-only)","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Extract checkerboard saddle points:\n\nsp = saddle_points(bundle, :pattern)              # Pixel units\nsp = saddle_points(bundle, :pattern; units=mm)     # Physical units","title":"saddle_points (Boards with :saddle frame only)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#World-Coordinates-(Boards-Only)","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Extract blobs in 3D calibration coordinates:\n\nb = blobs(bundle, :world)\n# Returns: Vector{Disc} with centered, y-up coordinates in unitless mm","title":"World Coordinates (Boards Only)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#File-I/O","page":"Quick Reference","text":"","title":"File I/O"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Loading","page":"Quick Reference","text":"bundle, image = load(\"blob_board.json\")   # From JSON\nbundle, image = load(\"blob_board.pdf\")    # From PDF\nbundle, image = load(\"blob_pattern.png\")  # From PNG","title":"Loading"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Saving","page":"Quick Reference","text":"# Boards (automatic during blob_board if dir provided)\nsave(figure, bundle; dir=\"./output\", format=\"pdf\")\n\n# Patterns (automatic during blob_pattern if dir provided)\nsave(pattern, bundle; dir=\"./output\")","title":"Saving"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Training-Pipeline","page":"Quick Reference","text":"","title":"Training Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#build_tracks_for_sequences-Track-Generation","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Detect the boards in every media item under a directory and accumulate their patch tracks into one .tracks file:\n\nout = build_tracks_for_sequences(board_dir, model_path, video_dir;\n    n_frames = 100,            # or stride=5\n    output_dir = \"./output\",   # writes <output_name>_<patch_type>_s<scale>.tracks\n    iou_threshold = 0.6,\n    patch_type = :logpolar,\n    scale = 32,\n    blur_sigma_max = 0,        # augmentations; 0 disables\n)\n\nSee Board Training Tracks for the full guide. The older build_tracks, which chained correspondences from relative .npy homographies, was removed in 2026-08.","title":"build_tracks_for_sequences - Track Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Loading-Tracks","page":"Quick Reference","text":".tracks is plain HDF5 (see examples/training/TRACKS_FORMAT.md); read it with HDF5.jl, h5py, or the Python helper blobboards.load_tracks.","title":"Loading Tracks"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#Critical-Unit-Distinction","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Pattern functions take blob_sizes in logical units (px):\n\nblob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing))  # px = pixels\n\nBoard functions take blob_sizes in physical units (mm, inch):\n\nblob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; blob_sizes=(4.0mm, nothing))  # mm = millimeters\n\nThe conversion happens automatically based on print_density; both are stored as σ in px (min_scale/max_scale).","title":"Critical Unit Distinction"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reference/#The-Board-JSON","page":"Quick Reference","text":"Every board is saved as a print file plus a JSON sidecar, blob_board_<uid>_<hash>.json:\n\n{\n  \"schema\": \"blobboards/board/v3\",\n  \"preamble\": {\n    \"board_config\":   { canvas_size, board_size, print_density, frame_width,\n                        frame_padding, frame_style,\n                        ruler_spec: { major_tick: { spacing, width },\n                                      minor_tick: { spacing, width } } },\n    \"pattern_config\": { pattern_resolution, min_scale, max_scale,   (σ, px)\n                        seed, pixel_type, scales_per_octave, max_trials,\n                        alpha, cutoff, raster_aligned, polarity }\n  },\n  \"blobs\":         { \"unit\": \"px\", \"centers\": […], \"sigmas\": […] },\n  \"saddle_points\": [ … ],                  (frame_style = \"saddle\"; else [])\n  \"hashes\":        { \"config\": \"<sha256 of the canonical preamble>\" },\n  \"render\":        { \"format\": \"pdf\", \"pdf_standard\": \"x4\", \"icc_profile\": null,\n                     \"id_label\": true }\n}\n\nLengths are { \"value\": …, \"unit\": \"mm\" | \"px\" | \"dpi\" }; a tick's width is its full drawn length across the frame band.\n\nIdentity is hashes.config, the SHA-256 of the canonical preamble; the file name and the printed uid are its first characters. It is stored, never recomputed on load.\nBlob sizes are stored only as σ in pattern pixels (min_scale, max_scale); blob_sizes and its units are an input convenience and are not recorded, so every spelling of one board mints one identity. The physical side lives in board_config (print_density, sizes in mm).\nrender records how the print file was made. It is not part of the identity: the same board with or without its id label has one hash. blob_board(json) and the gallery's regeneration render as recorded; a file without the block renders as PDF/X-4 with no label.\nOlder schemas still load. v1 files (with max_diameter_fraction) have the fraction converted to the largest σ it produced, so v1 boards re-place bit-identically. v1 and v2 rulers carry a ruler_spec.width and half-length tick widths; the reader drops the one and doubles the others, which is the ruler those boards were drawn with. Their stored hashes.config stays their identity.","title":"The Board JSON"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Board-Training-Tracks","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"build_tracks_for_sequences is the current driver for generating descriptor training data from calibration boards. It takes a directory of media (videos and/or photos), a directory of board .json files, and an ONNX descriptor model, and accumulates multi-modal, multi-scale patch tracks into a single .tracks (HDF5) file.\n\nnote: How observations are keyed\nbuild_tracks_for_sequences detects the board in each frame independently and transfers the board's ground-truth blobs through the estimated world→image homography, so every observation of a blob is keyed by its board-relative identity rather than by a chain of pairwise matches. Tracks therefore survive occlusion and frame gaps.An earlier build_tracks chained correspondences frame-to-frame from relative GT homographies in a .npy file. It was removed in 2026-08 along with merge_tracks, the Vector{Track} HDF5 layout and its reader; this is now the only track builder.","title":"Board Training Tracks"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Quick-Start","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"using BlobBoards\n\nout = build_tracks_for_sequences(\n    \"/data/boards\",          # board .json files\n    \"/data/models/blob_matcher.onnx\",\n    \"/data/videos\";          # videos and/or photos\n    stride = 10,\n    max_detections = 5000,   # see \"Detection cap\" below — set this on 4K+ footage\n    output_dir = \"/data/out\",\n    output_name = \"tracks\",\n)\n# => \"/data/out/tracks.tracks\"\n\nRun with julia --threads=N (e.g. 16). Two stages use the extra threads: the per-frame correspondence search, and the async HDF5 writer.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#What-It-Produces","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"For each media item × board that is successfully detected, one sequence named <media>_<uid>, plus one anchor sequence per board named by the bare board uid.\n\nSequence Name Contents\nTracked <media>_<uid> Observations of each GT blob found in the media, 1 obs per frame it was matched in\nAnchor <uid> Every GT reference blob rendered from the board image, exactly 1 obs each\n\nAnchor and tracked sequences share track ids (board_offset + i, where board_offset is derived from the board preamble hash), so a track's ground-truth reference patch is recoverable by id. Anchors are not seeded into the tracked sequences.\n\nEach observation stores three patch views — log_polar, cartesian, and original — at every scale in scales, plus the observation's affine_shape. Cartesian and log-polar are sampled off the adapted elliptical frame; original is sampled off the raw SIFT-detection disc, so its scale is relative to the detector's σ rather than the fitted σ.","title":"What It Produces"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Pipeline","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Per media item:\n\nRead — videos are sampled by n_frames/stride; photos are a single frame. Frames over max_image_pixels are downscaled at read time (see below).\nTrack boards — track_boards detects and tracks every board across the frames, yielding a per-frame world→image homography and a fit quality.\nAugment — optional resolution-loss resample and/or perspective warp.\nDetect + extract — one detect_blobs → adapt → filter_blobs → canonicalize call per frame, shared across all boards in that frame.\nCorrespond — the board's GT world blobs are transferred through the homography and validated by symmetric IoU (iou_threshold).\nSave — tracks with ≥1 observation are kept; unmatched detections become confusers, capped and randomly subsampled.\n\nStep 5 runs once per frame, not once per (frame, board): a frame containing four boards is extracted once and matched against all four.","title":"Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Augmentation","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Two augmentations may fire independently on each frame, both before extraction.","title":"Augmentation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Resolution-loss","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"With probability p_downsample (default 0.75) the frame is down- then up-sampled (0.5 / 0.25 / 0.125×) to simulate blur and scale loss. The resample preserves image coordinates, so the homography stays valid. Conditional on firing, the three tiers (0.5× / 0.25× / 0.125×) are equally likely.","title":"Resolution loss"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Perspective","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"With probability p_perspective the frame is warped by a homography that strengthens the board's real foreshortening, rather than applying a perspective-agnostic random warp.\n\nA board's world→image homography already encodes the plane's foreshortening: its projective (bottom) row is the board plane's vanishing line in board coordinates. Scaling that row by 1+γ amplifies the existing foreshortening along the board's own tilt direction; a small random-direction tilt adds pose diversity on top. Magnitudes are applied in a board-normalized frame (world extent → [-1,1]²), so they are unit- and board-size-invariant.\n\nThe board centroid is a fixed point of the warp — the world center maps to the normalized origin, whose image is the third column of the normalized homography, untouched by both the row-scaling and the jitter — so the board stays put in the frame while only its foreshortening changes.\n\nOne warp applies to the whole frame, so it is anchored to the primary (highest-quality) board; the other boards ride along, and every board's world→image homography is composed with the same H_aug, keeping all correspondences valid.\n\nWarp-void clip filter. A warp pulls image content in from outside the frame, leaving a fill void inside the array. filter_blobs checks each core ellipse against the full array and is blind to that interior boundary, so when a warp fires each ellipse is additionally pulled back through inv(H_aug) and checked against the original rectangle with the same max_clip_ratio=0.2 — treating the void exactly like the real image edge. It is a no-op on unwarped frames.","title":"Perspective"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Anti-Aliasing","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"supersample is forwarded to canonicalize for every view and scale. At the large scales (64/96/128) one output texel spans many source pixels, so the default single-tap sampling aliases badly at the outer radii — and because the resulting moiré depends on subpixel position, it is not repeatable across views of the same blob. supersample > 1 samples on a finer grid and box-averages down.\n\nCost scales as supersample² and is paid at every scale, including the small ones that do not alias. 3–4 is the quality sweet spot.","title":"Anti-Aliasing"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Keyword-Arguments","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"","title":"Keyword Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Sampling","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Parameter Default Description\nn_frames nothing Uniformly sample N frames (stride computed)\nstride nothing Process every stride-th frame\nmax_image_pixels 3840*2160 Cap on total pixels per frame; nothing = native\n\nn_frames and stride are mutually exclusive; neither = every frame. Both are ignored for photos.\n\nmax_image_pixels is a pixel budget, not a longest-side cap, so square frames are tamed too. It is required for high-resolution stills: the Vulkan SIFT detector allocates a full image pyramid sized to the frame and OOMs on e.g. a 24 MP phone photo. 4K footage is unaffected. Applied at read time, so detect / track / adapt / canonicalize all share one coordinate system.","title":"Sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Filtering","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Parameter Default Description\niou_threshold 0.3 Minimum symmetric IoU for a valid correspondence\nmax_detections typemax(Int) Per-frame detection cap\n\nQuality filter. A spurious board's fitted homography does not explain the image, so its quality goes negative, whereas genuine detections score positive — 0.0 sits on the sign boundary. Transferring a spurious board's GT blobs would inject wrong correspondences into the training set. Note that inlier and matched counts do not separate the two cases (a weak genuine detection can have fewer inliers than a spurious one), which is why the filter is on quality.\n\nDetection cap. Set max_detections (e.g. 5000) on high-resolution footage. Without it the GPU descriptor matcher runs a detections × board-features GEMM that can exhaust GPU memory — a Sony FX3 frame yields ~50k detections.","title":"Filtering"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Patches","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Parameter Default Description\nscales [4,8,16,32,64,96,128] Patch scales = half patch width/height in σ\nsupersample 1 Anti-aliasing supersample factor (see above)\nconfuser_factor 1.0 Cap confusers at factor × tracked patches\n\nEach confuser gets one randomly chosen scale; its log-polar and cartesian views are stored under untracked/. All confusers are collected in memory per board-sequence before the random subsample — lower max_detections if memory is tight.","title":"Patches"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Augmentation-2","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Parameter Default Description\np_downsample 0.75 Probability of the resolution-loss down/up-resample; 0 disables\np_perspective 0.5 Probability of the perspective warp; 0 disables\nperspective_strengthen_max 2.0 γ ~ U[0, max]; vanishing line scaled by 1+γ\nperspective_jitter_max 0.1 Extra random-direction tilt, board-normalized\n\nperspective_strengthen_max = 0 reproduces the frame's real perspective; larger values exaggerate it along the board's own tilt.","title":"Augmentation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Output","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Parameter Default Description\noutput_dir \".\" Directory for $output_name.tracks\noutput_name \"tracks\" Output file basename\nwrite_anchors true Write the per-board GT anchor sequences\nasync_writer true Overlap HDF5 compression with GPU compute\nwriter_buffer 1 Depth of the async-writer queue\nbackend CudaBackend() Compute backend for adapt/canonicalize/describe","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Resume","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Sequences already present in the output file are skipped — both <media>_<uid> tracked sequences and <uid> anchor sequences — so an interrupted run can simply be re-invoked with the same arguments.","title":"Resume"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Async-Writer","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"zlib compression is single-threaded and dominates wall-clock on anchor galleries, leaving the GPU idle. With async_writer=true a single background task owns the output file and performs every HDF5 write, fed by a bounded channel; the GPU thread hands off each finished sequence and proceeds to the next while compression runs concurrently.\n\nOne writer = one file owner, which is required: concurrent writers to a single HDF5 file corrupt it. Real overlap needs julia -t ≥2; under -t 1 it degrades to serial (still correct). Set async_writer=false for synchronous writes on the GPU thread — an escape hatch for -t 1 and memory-tight runs.\n\nwriter_buffer trades memory for slack: larger values let the GPU thread run further ahead but hold more finished (large) sequences in memory. 0 is a rendezvous.\n\nSince the workload is compression-bound, the writer overlaps GPU time behind zlib but cannot speed zlib itself — a faster codec (LZ4/Blosc) is the complementary lever.","title":"Async Writer"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Performance-Notes","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Threading: only symmetric_iou_match parallelizes (pure-CPU ellipse/IoU over the frame's detections). HDF5 compression and the media loop do not.\nMedia-level parallelism is not available via threads. VulkanSift holds a single global Vulkan instance, and its handle is evicted and destroyed on any image-size change, so concurrent detection from multiple threads races on that global. Per-thread ONNX sessions do not unlock it. True concurrent detection needs separate processes, each of which replicates the board gallery and CUDA context.\nwrite_anchors=false is a large speedup for diagnostics that only need the tracked sequences — anchor galleries are big and compression-bound.\nCUDA/Vulkan memory: the driver calls GC.gc(); CUDA.reclaim() between media items so the growing CUDA pool leaves physical GPU memory for Vulkan's pyramid init.\nProgress output: Julia buffers println to redirected files, so progress prints only flush on exit. Watch the output .tracks file (HDF5 flushes on write) as the live success signal.","title":"Performance Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#HDF5-Layout","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"One file holds a single patch type at a single scale (both are recorded as tracks-group attributes and encoded in the file name — e.g. tracks_logpolar_s32.tracks).\n\n/sequences/\n    <media>_<uid>/                  # tracked sequence\n        tracks/                     # @attr patch_type (String), scale (Float64)\n            patches               — (P, P, N) Float32, chunked + compressed\n            masks                 — (P, P, N) Float32  # board ∩ in-frame, per pixel\n            affine_shapes         — (2, 2, N) Float32\n            frame_id              — (N,) Int32   # source frame of each observation\n            homographies          — (3, 3, F) Float64  # one H_wi per detected frame\n            view_angles           — (F,) Float64        # per-frame obliquity, radians\n            homography_frame_ids  — (F,) Int32   # frame index of each H_wi column\n            n_visible             — (F,) Int32   # board GT blobs inside the frame\n            n_detections          — (F,) Int32   # blobs detected in that frame\n            track_id              — (N,) Int32\n            n_tracks              — Int32 scalar\n            track_lengths         — (T,) Int32\n        untracked/                  # confusers\n            patches               — (P, P, M) Float32\n            masks                 — (P, P, M) Float32\n    <uid>/                          # anchor sequence — no untracked/, no frame_id,\n        tracks/...                  # and no homographies/view_angles (GT reference)\n\nDetection geometry is stored per frame, not per blob: homographies holds one estimated world→image homography H_wi (including any perspective augmentation) for each of the F frames the board was detected in, and view_angles the matching viewing obliquity in radians — derived intrinsics-free from the local affine tilt t = σ_max/σ_min of H_wi at the board centroid (acos(1/t); 0 = fronto-parallel, π/2 = edge-on), metric because the board is physically square. homography_frame_ids gives the frame index of each column, so an observation joins to its pose via frame_id[k] → the matching entry of homography_frame_ids. n_visible counts the board's own GT blobs that project inside the frame — the honest denominator for how many it matched — and n_detections how many blobs the detector kept anywhere in the frame. All six are written for tracked sequences only; GT-anchor sequences omit them. (One H_wi per board per frame assumes at most one detection of a board per frame, which track_boards' spatial suppression enforces.)\n\nHDF5.jl reverses dimensions on write (Julia column-major → HDF5 row-major), so h5py reads patches as (N, P, P), homographies as (F, 3, 3) and view_angles/homography_frame_ids/n_visible/n_detections as (F,) directly.","title":"HDF5 Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Detection-Yield-vs.-Viewing-Angle","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Track construction charts how the per-board blob yield falls off as a board tilts away from fronto-parallel. build_tracks_for_sequences writes <output_name>_view_angles.png next to the .tracks file at the end of a run, and build_absolute_tracks writes a per-media <media>_view_angles.png into debug_dir when one is set. Rendering needs a Makie backend — using CairoMakie is the headless choice, and examples/training/build_tracks.jl loads it for you. Without a backend the same binning prints as a terminal bar chart instead.\n\nThree panels share one obliquity axis:\n\nPanel Shows Read it for\nmatched blobs / board-frame mean ±1 sd the raw yield — what a training batch gets\nrecall Σ matched / Σ visible GT yield with board-in-frame size divided out\nboard-frames samples per bin which bars carry enough support to believe\n\nOne sample is one (board, frame) detection. matched is the board's GT blobs that won a bidirectional-IoU correspondence. visible is its GT blobs whose centers project inside the frame — this is what recall divides by, and it falls as the board leaves the frame, so a partly-out-of-frame board doesn't read as a detector failure. The separately recorded n_detections is frame-level and board-agnostic (every board in a frame shares it), so it is context, not a denominator.\n\nNothing extra is stored for the chart — it is rebuilt from view_angles, n_visible and per-observation frame_id counts, so it is recomputable offline:\n\nstats = view_angle_profile(\"tracks_logpolar_s32.tracks\")     # records, no plotting\nsave_view_angle_figure(\"profile.png\", stats)                 # needs CairoMakie\nsave_view_angle_figure(\"fine.svg\", stats; edges = 0:5:90)    # finer bins, vector out\nsave_view_angle_figure(\"one_board.png\", filter(s -> s.uid == uid, stats))\nprint_view_angle_profile(stats)                              # text, no backend needed\n\nFiles built before n_visible existed still profile; the recall panel says so rather than drawing an empty one.\n\nThe legacy Vector{Track} save path instead writes a single untracked/patches dataset; see the Public API training section.","title":"Detection Yield vs. Viewing Angle"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/board-tracks/#Known-Issues","page":"Board Training Tracks","text":"Vulkan SIFT logs ~25-30k feature(s) lost because the SIFT buffer was full on 8 MP photos — the detector's internal buffer caps below the detected count (before max_detections applies). This biases which blobs survive; raise the SIFT buffer size if completeness matters. Non-fatal.\nDomainError: sqrt(-1.5e-11) from RansacScoringDoneRight._mahalanobis during RANSAC is caught per-board by find_board (\"skipping this board for this frame\"). Rare, upstream, non-fatal.","title":"Known Issues"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Python-Interface","page":"Python Interface","text":"The blobboards Python package wraps this Julia package through juliacall. It has one function per Julia entry point, with the same names, keywords and semantics, and with NumPy arrays and dataclasses in place of Julia types. Each Python function's docstring (help(blobboards.blob_board)) is its full reference: parameters, defaults, units, return fields, errors and an example. This page covers installation and tasks.","title":"Python Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Installation","page":"Python Interface","text":"","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#With-pixi-(recommended)","page":"Python Interface","text":"pixi install            # Julia tools, Ghostscript, Python, the blobboards package\npixi run -e py python","title":"With pixi (recommended)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Manual-setup","page":"Python Interface","text":"Requirements:\n\nPython 3.8+\nJulia 1.12+ (juliacall uses it)\nGhostscript ≥ 10.07 to write the PDF/X-4 print master. pixi provides it; distribution packages are often older (Ubuntu 24.04 ships 10.02, which is refused with an error).\n\ncd python\npip install -e .        # juliacall, numpy; `pip install pint matplotlib` for units and plots\n\nThe package runs Julia in the checkout's authoring/ environment. That environment provides CairoMakie, Libtiff_jll and PythonCall, plus CUDA and cuDNN, which are loaded only when detection first runs. The package activates it on first use; the first call can take minutes on a fresh machine.","title":"Manual setup"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Functions","page":"Python Interface","text":"Python Julia What it does\nblob_board(paper, dpi, **kw) blob_board a printable board (PDF/X, TIFF or PNG + JSON)\nblob_pattern(width, height, **kw) blob_pattern a blob pattern in pixels\nload_board(json_path) load_board / BlobBoards.load_bundle a stored board, under its stored identity\nrender_board(json_path, dir=…, **kw) BlobBoards.render_board re-render a stored board without re-placing it\nblobs(json_path, …), saddle_points(json_path, …) BlobBoards.blobs, BlobBoards.saddle_points coordinates in a frame (pattern, board, canvas, world) and unit\nfind_boards(board_paths, model_path, img, …) find_boards boards in an image: homography or pose per board\ntrack_boards(board_paths, model_path, frames, …) track_boards boards through a video or a frame sequence\ndevice(requested=\"auto\") default_device where detection runs: \"cuda\" or \"cpu\"\nexample_data(name) BlobBoards.example_data the bundled example boards, still and clip\ndescriptor_network_path(config=None) descriptor_network_path the descriptor network a run configuration names\nPRINT_SIZES BlobBoards.PRINT_SIZES paper names → (width, height) in mm\nshort_side, ShortSideFraction short_side, BlobBoards.ShortSideFraction a blob size as a fraction of the pattern's short side\nBoardResult, BoardPreamble, BoardConfig, PatternResult, PatternConfig BoardBundle, BoardPreamble, BoardConfig, …, PatternConfig results and configurations\n\nTwo Julia options have no Python form: the radially distorted geometry (RadialHomographyGeometry) and the leading pixel-type argument of blob_board / blob_pattern (patterns and boards are 8-bit grey from Python).","title":"Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Quick-start","page":"Python Interface","text":"from blobboards import blob_pattern, blob_board\n\npattern = blob_pattern(800, 600, blob_sizes=(12, None), seed=0x1234)   # discs from 12 px across\nprint(pattern.pattern.shape, pattern.blobs.shape)                     # (600, 800) (N, 3): x, y, σ\n\nboard = blob_board(\"A4\", 150, blob_sizes=(4.0, None), seed=0x1234,     # discs from 4 mm across\n                   frame_style=\"saddle\", dir=\"./output\")\nprint(board.uid, board.blobs.shape, board.saddle_points.shape)\n\nblob_sizes are the smallest and largest disc diameters: mm for boards (bare numbers; Pint quantities in any length unit work too), px for patterns. Board-generation keywords mirror Julia's (alpha is Julia's α). A keyword Julia removed (blob_measure, ruler_width, raster_aligned on boards) is not a parameter, so giving it raises TypeError.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Finding-boards","page":"Python Interface","text":"find_boards(board_paths, model_path, img, *, gallery_path, camera_json=None, device=\"auto\") returns one dict per verified board:\n\nuid\nmodel: a 3×3 homography from the board's world plane (mm) to pixels, or {\"R\", \"t\"}, the board → camera pose, when camera_json is given\nquality, n_inliers, n_csponds, weights (a NumPy array)\ndevice and execution_provider: where it ran, \"cuda\" or \"cpu\"\n\nThe network, the gallery features (cached on disk at gallery_path) and the bundles are built once per process and reused.","title":"Finding boards"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#GPU-or-CPU","page":"Python Interface","text":"device=\"auto\" (the default) runs on the GPU when a usable NVIDIA GPU is present. Otherwise it runs on the CPU and logs a warning on the blobboards logger.\n\ndevice=\"cuda\" raises RuntimeError when there is no usable GPU.\ndevice=\"cpu\" always uses the CPU.\n\nThe detection stages and the descriptor network (ONNX Runtime's CUDA or CPU provider) run on the same device. CUDA is loaded only when detection first asks for a device, so blob_board never pays for it. To see where detection will run without running it:\n\nimport blobboards\nblobboards.device()          # \"cuda\" or \"cpu\", as \"auto\" resolves on this machine\n\nThe GPU requirements are those of the Julia side (see Detection and Pose):\n\nan NVIDIA GPU of compute capability 7.5 or newer\na driver for CUDA 12 or newer\nLinux or Windows\n\nCUDA, cuDNN and the CUDA 12.9 runtime come as Julia artifacts with the package's Julia environment, so no CUDA toolkit is needed. Choose a GPU with CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES before starting Python; CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"\" forces the CPU.\n\nOn a B200 with the example still (12 MP, 50 boards), a warm call took 1.8–1.9 s per image on the GPU and 16–17 s on the CPU. The first call also builds the gallery: about 2 minutes, or seconds once its caches are warm.\n\nimport os\nimport numpy as np\nfrom blobboards import find_boards, example_data, descriptor_network_path\n\nboards = example_data(\"boards\")\nboard_paths = sorted(os.path.join(boards, f) for f in os.listdir(boards) if f.endswith(\".json\"))\nmodel_path = descriptor_network_path()\n# img: a grey image, (H, W) uint8 or float in [0, 1]\nfound = find_boards(board_paths, model_path, img, gallery_path=\"gallery.jld2\")\nfor b in found:\n    print(b[\"uid\"], b[\"n_inliers\"], b[\"device\"], b[\"execution_provider\"])","title":"GPU or CPU"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Checking-the-result-visually","page":"Python Interface","text":"Each homography maps its board's world plane (mm) to image pixels, so the board's blobs can be drawn straight onto the image with matplotlib:\n\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom blobboards import blobs\n\nuid_to_json = {os.path.basename(p)[11:14]: p for p in board_paths}   # blob_board_<uid>_….json\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 9.5))\nax.imshow(img, cmap=\"gray\")\nfor b in found:\n    X = blobs(uid_to_json[b[\"uid\"]], reference_frame=\"world\", units=\"mm\")   # (N, 3): x, y, σ\n    p = np.c_[X[:, :2], np.ones(len(X))] @ b[\"model\"].T                    # homogeneous pixels\n    ax.plot(p[:, 0] / p[:, 2], p[:, 1] / p[:, 2], \".\", ms=2)\n    ax.annotate(b[\"uid\"], p[0, :2] / p[0, 2], color=\"yellow\")\nfig.savefig(\"found_boards.png\", dpi=100)","title":"Checking the result visually"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Video","page":"Python Interface","text":"track_boards takes a video path, or any sequence of grey frames. It returns, per board uid, one dict per frame the board was found in: frame, model, quality, n_inliers and source (\"track\", \"init\" or \"detect\").\n\nfrom blobboards import track_boards\n\nframes = [img, img]                       # or a video path, e.g. in example_data(\"video\")\ntracks = track_boards(board_paths, model_path, frames, gallery_path=\"gallery.jld2\")\nfor uid, dets in tracks.items():\n    print(uid, len(dets), \"frames\")","title":"Video"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Troubleshooting","page":"Python Interface","text":"could not load library \".../lib/julia/sys.so\" at import: juliacall was given juliaup's launcher instead of a Julia binary. Point it at the real one: export PYTHON_JULIAPKG_EXE=$(julia -e 'print(joinpath(Sys.BINDIR, \"julia\"))').\nUnless you set them yourself, the package sets PYTHON_JULIACALL_HANDLE_SIGNALS=yes and PYTHON_JULIACALL_THREADS=auto before juliacall is first imported. If Python handles Julia's signals, GPU work crashes the process. Import blobboards before anything else that imports juliacall.","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Stored-boards","page":"Python Interface","text":"A board's JSON sidecar is its definition. load_board reads it, in every schema BlobBoards has written, under the stored identity. render_board draws it again from its recorded blobs:\n\nimport tempfile\nfrom blobboards import load_board, render_board\n\nboard = load_board(board_paths[0])\nprint(board.uid, board.preamble.board_config.print_density_dpi)\nrender_board(board_paths[0], dir=tempfile.mkdtemp(), format=\"png\")","title":"Stored boards"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Coordinates","page":"Python Interface","text":"from blobboards import blob_board, blobs, saddle_points\n\nout = tempfile.mkdtemp()\nblob_board(\"A5\", 100, frame_style=\"saddle\", seed=1, dir=out)\njson_path = [os.path.join(out, f) for f in os.listdir(out) if f.endswith(\".json\")][0]\n\nb = blobs(json_path)                                          # px, pattern frame, COLMAP convention\nb_mm = blobs(json_path, units=\"mm\", reference_frame=\"board\")  # mm, board frame\nw = blobs(json_path, units=\"mm\", reference_frame=\"world\")     # the board plane: centred, y up\nsp = saddle_points(json_path)                                 # (M, 2), saddle frames only\nb_cv = blobs(json_path, image_origin=\"opencv\")                # for NumPy/OpenCV sampling\n\nimage_origin First pixel centre Use\n\"colmap\" (0.5, 0.5) COLMAP/SfM, geometry (the default for coordinates)\n\"opencv\" (0, 0) NumPy/OpenCV image sampling (the default for detection results)\n\"julia\" (1, 1) Julia/MATLAB\n\nreference_frame Origin Description\n\"pattern\" Pattern top-left Excludes the frame (default)\n\"board\" Board top-left Includes the frame\n\"canvas\" Page top-left Includes the margins\n\"world\" Board centre, y up, mm What a find_boards homography maps to pixels","title":"Coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Pint-units","page":"Python Interface","text":"Lengths can be Pint quantities in any unit; bare numbers are mm for boards and px for patterns:\n\nimport pint\nfrom blobboards import short_side\nureg = pint.UnitRegistry()\n\nboard = blob_board(\"A4\", 300, blob_sizes=(6.0 * ureg.mm, None),\n                   frame_width=0.5 * ureg.inch, frame_padding=2 * ureg.mm)\nboard = blob_board(\"A4\", 300, blob_sizes=(4.0, 0.2 * short_side))   # largest disc 20% of the short side","title":"Pint units"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Blob-detection-only","page":"Python Interface","text":"detect_blobs exposes the detector alone (advanced API; see its docstring):\n\nfrom blobboards import detect_blobs\n\nresult = detect_blobs(np.random.rand(240, 320))\nprint(len(result), result.to_array().shape)    # (N, 3): x, y, σ","title":"Blob detection only"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/python/#Training-tracks","page":"Python Interface","text":".tracks files are written from Julia by build_tracks_for_sequences; there is no Python builder. Reading them needs no Julia (h5py only): load_tracks(path) returns a TrackData, and plot_tracks(data, max_tracks=10) plots the longest tracks.","title":"Training tracks"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"This guide walks through the basics of generating blob patterns and calibration boards.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#With-pixi-(recommended)","page":"Getting Started","text":"Pixi handles all dependencies (Julia, Python, Ghostscript) automatically:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/BlobBoards.jl\ncd BlobBoards.jl\npixi install\npixi run julia --project=.","title":"With pixi (recommended)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Manual-setup","page":"Getting Started","text":"Requirements:\n\nJulia 1.12+\nGhostscript ≥ 10.07 (writes the PDF/X-4 print master; pixi provides it — distro packages are often older, e.g. Ubuntu 24.04 ships 10.02, which is refused loudly)\n\nInstall Ghostscript:\n\nUbuntu/Debian: sudo apt install ghostscript\nmacOS: brew install ghostscript\nFedora: sudo dnf install ghostscript\nWindows: Download from ghostscript.com\n\nThen install Julia packages:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/BlobBoards.jl\ncd BlobBoards.jl\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'","title":"Manual setup"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Author-your-first-board","page":"Getting Started","text":"Board authoring needs CairoMakie, a weak dependency that the package's own --project=. cannot load. Use the checkout's authoring/ environment:\n\njulia --project=authoring -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=authoring\n\nusing BlobBoards, CairoMakie\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; seed = 0xBEEF, dir = \"./output\")\n\nThis writes blob_board_<uid>.pdf (print it at 100%) and its JSON sidecar. The Authoring a Board guide covers the environment, sizes, frames and rulers, output formats and printing; blob_board lists every keyword.","title":"Author your first board"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Detect-boards-and-estimate-pose","page":"Getting Started","text":"find_boards finds known boards in an image and returns each one's homography or pose. See Detection and Pose.","title":"Detect boards and estimate pose"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Generating-Patterns-Only","page":"Getting Started","text":"For training data or custom layouts, generate just the pattern:\n\nusing BlobBoards\nusing VisualGeometryCore: px\n\n# Generate a 800x600 pixel pattern\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), seed=0x1234)\n\n# Save to disk\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), dir=\"./output\")","title":"Generating Patterns Only"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Extracting-Blob-Coordinates","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Extracting Blob Coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#From-Board-Files","page":"Getting Started","text":"bundle, image = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Physical coordinates (mm) relative to pattern origin\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern; units=mm)\n\n# Physical coordinates relative to board origin (includes frame)\nb = blobs(bundle, :board; units=mm)\n\n# Pixel coordinates (COLMAP convention, convert as needed)\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern)","title":"From Board Files"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#From-Pattern-Files","page":"Getting Started","text":"bundle = load(\"blob_pattern.json\")\n\n# Pixel coordinates (COLMAP convention)\nb = blobs(bundle)","title":"From Pattern Files"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#World-Coordinates-for-3D-Calibration","page":"Getting Started","text":"bundle, image = load(\"blob_board.json\")\n\n# Get blobs in world frame (centered, y-up, unitless mm)\nworld = blobs(bundle, :world)\n\n# Lift to 3D points on z=0 plane\nusing GeometryBasics: Point3\nX = [Point3(b.center..., 0.0) for b in blobs]","title":"World Coordinates for 3D Calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Loading-Saved-Boards","page":"Getting Started","text":"# Load from any paired file (JSON, PNG, or PDF)\nbundle, image = load(\"blob_board_abc_12345.json\")\nbundle, image = load(\"blob_board_abc_12345.pdf\")\n\n# Access configuration\nconfig = pattern_config(bundle)\nboard = board_config(bundle)","title":"Loading Saved Boards"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Available-Paper-Sizes","page":"Getting Started","text":"Standard sizes include:\n\nISO ANSI Common Names\nA0-A5 ANSI A-E1 Letter, Legal\n  Tabloid, Ledger\n\n# List all available sizes\nkeys(BlobBoards.PRINT_SIZES)","title":"Available Paper Sizes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Next-Steps","page":"Getting Started","text":"Patterns Guide - Detailed pattern generation options\nAuthoring a Board - Board layout, output formats and printing\nDetection and Pose - find_boards, track_boards and the GPU environment\nCoordinates Guide - Coordinate system details\nAPI Reference - Complete function reference","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/boards/#Board-API","page":"Boards","text":"","title":"Board API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/boards/#Main-Functions","page":"Boards","text":"See Public API for blob_board.","title":"Main Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/boards/#Coordinate-Functions","page":"Boards","text":"Coordinate transforms are extensions of the CoordinateTransformations constructors:\n\n# Reference-frame translations (within the board layout)\nT = Translation(board_config, :pattern, :canvas)   # BoardConfig: :pattern/:board/:canvas\n\n# Coordinate-system transforms (pixel ↔ physical ↔ world)\nH = AffineMap(bundle, :logical, :world)            # :logical/:physical/:world\n\n# Image-origin conventions (from VisualGeometryCore)\nT_origin = Translation(:colmap, :julia)            # :colmap/:julia/:opencv\n\n# Compose freely\nT_full = Translation(:colmap, :julia) ∘ Translation(board_config(bundle), :pattern, :canvas)\n\nSee also Public API for blobs (including blobs(bundle, :world)) and saddle_points.","title":"Coordinate Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/boards/#Internal-Functions","page":"Boards","text":"","title":"Internal Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/boards/#QR-Code-Generation","page":"Boards","text":"make_qr_code(preamble; error_correction) - Generate QR code matrix from board metadata","title":"QR Code Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Public-API","page":"Public API","text":"This page lists the public surface of BlobBoards.jl. Exported names are listed bare; public (supported, unexported) names are listed qualified and are reached as BlobBoards.name.","title":"Public API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Pattern-Generation","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Pattern Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Board-Generation","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Board Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Blob-and-Feature-Extraction","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Blob and Feature Extraction"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Detection-and-Pose","page":"Public API","text":"find_boards is the entry point: it detects blobs, fits elliptical frames, canonicalizes patches, describes them, matches against a board gallery, and estimates pose. The camera lives inside config.geometry — PoseGeometry for the calibrated P3P path, HomographyGeometry for the uncalibrated one — so a camera/geometry mismatch is unrepresentable rather than a runtime error.\n\nAdaptation is backend-generic (KernelAbstractions): device = KA.CPU() runs the threaded host implementation, CUDABackend() (or any KA GPU backend) the device kernels. Results agree to within the pipeline's own run-to-run jitter; CUDA additionally provides cuBLAS/libdevice fast paths.","title":"Detection and Pose"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#The-staged-gallery","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"The staged gallery"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Pipeline-stages","page":"Public API","text":"Each stage is usable on its own when you want to inspect or replace part of the chain.","title":"Pipeline stages"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Descriptors-and-the-board-gallery","page":"Public API","text":"BoardGallery caches per-board features on disk. It stores the descriptor model's hash, so switching networks invalidates the cache and it rebuilds.","title":"Descriptors and the board gallery"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Non-maximum-suppression","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Non-maximum suppression"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Board-Geometry","page":"Public API","text":"Use Rect(bundle, reference_frame) to get bounding rectangles in different coordinate systems:\n\nusing GeometryBasics: Rect\n\n# Pattern coords (y-down, origin at pattern top-left)\nrect = Rect(bundle, :pattern)  # Pattern area only\nrect = Rect(bundle, :board)    # Full board (includes frame)\nrect = Rect(bundle, :canvas)   # Full canvas (includes margins)\n\n# World coords (y-up, centered at origin)\nrect = Rect(bundle, :world)    # For 3D visualization\n\nThe :world frame returns a centered rect with unitless mm values, suitable for 3D visualization where the board origin is at the centroid.","title":"Board Geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Coordinate-Transforms","page":"Public API","text":"BlobBoards extends CoordinateTransformations constructors:\n\nTranslation(bc::BoardConfig, from, to) — reference-frame translations (:pattern/:board/:canvas)\nAffineMap(bundle::BoardBundle, from, to; image_origin) — coordinate-system transforms (:logical/:physical/:world)\n\nFor image-origin conventions (:colmap/:julia/:opencv), use Translation(:colmap, :julia) (defined in VisualGeometryCore — returns a px-unit-bearing Translation).","title":"Coordinate Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Unit-Conversion-Helpers","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Unit Conversion Helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Training-Pipeline","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Training Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#File-I/O","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"File I/O"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#.tracks-files","page":"Public API","text":".tracks is plain HDF5 and is not registered with FileIO — the format\"BlobBoardTracks\" hooks were removed in 2026-08 (the format had in fact never been registered, so FileIO.load(\"x.tracks\") never worked). Write them with build_tracks_for_sequences; read them with HDF5.jl, with h5py, or with the Python helper blobboards.load_tracks. The layout is documented in examples/training/TRACKS_FORMAT.md.","title":".tracks files"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Units","page":"Public API","text":"The following units are re-exported for convenience:\n\nUnit Description\npx Pixels (logical)\nmm Millimeters\ncm Centimeters\nm Meters\ninch Inches\nft Feet\ndpi Dots per inch\npt Points (1/72 inch)\nμm Micrometers","title":"Units"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Types","page":"Public API","text":"The main types used in BlobBoards are:\n\nSize2 - Width/height dimensions (from VisualGeometryCore)\nDisc - Isotropic blob/feature type (from VisualGeometryCore)\nPatternConfig - Pattern generation configuration\nBoardConfig - Board layout configuration\nPatternBundle - Pattern with metadata (returned by blob_pattern)\nBoardBundle - Board with metadata (returned by blob_board)","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/public/#Media-enumeration","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Media enumeration"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.blob_pattern","page":"Public API","text":"blob_pattern(config::PatternConfig; dir=nothing)\n\nGenerate a deterministic blob pattern from a PatternConfig. RNG is MersenneTwister(config.seed) for reproducible output.\n\nReturns (pattern, blobs) where pattern is the rendered image and blobs is a vector of placed blobs. If dir is provided, saves the pattern as PNG with JSON sidecar.\n\n\n\n\n\nblob_pattern(pixel_type::Type, pattern_resolution::Size2; kwargs...)\n\nGenerate a blob pattern with specified pixel type and resolution.\n\nArguments\n\npixel_type: Output pixel type (e.g., Gray{Float64}, Gray{N0f16})\npattern_resolution: Pattern size as Size2 with logical units\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nblob_sizes: (min, max), the smallest and largest disc DIAMETER (2·cutoff·σ) in logical units (12px; a bare number is px). max may be 0.2short_side (a fraction of the short side) or nothing (default: 20% of the short side). The default min is 2·cutoff·2px (8 px at the default cutoff), which is also the floor (σ ≥ 2 px). Sizes are stored as σ in px (min_scale, max_scale), both exact; the sizes between are spaced evenly in log at about 1/scales_per_octave octave (see calc_blob_scales).\nseed: Random seed for reproducible patterns\nscales_per_octave: Number of scales per octave (default 3)\nα: Scale sampling bias (default 1.2)\ncutoff: Gaussian truncation radius in units of σ (default DEFAULT_CUTOFF = 2; use it)\nmax_trials: Maximum placement trials per blob (default 5000)\nraster_aligned: Align blob centers to raster grid (default true)\npolarity: Blob polarity, :dark (default) or :light\ndir: Output directory for saving files (optional)\n\nReturns (pattern, blobs, config).\n\n\n\n\n\nblob_pattern(width, height; kwargs...)\n\nGenerate a blob pattern with specified dimensions in pixels.\n\nArguments\n\nwidth, height: Pattern dimensions in pixels (Integer) or logical units (px quantities)\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nblob_sizes: disc diameter range (see the pixel_type form)\nOther arguments same as blob_pattern(pixel_type, pattern_resolution; kwargs...), except the pixel type: it is positional, so supply blob_pattern(T, Size2(…)) (a pixel_type keyword is an ArgumentError)\n\nReturns (pattern, blobs, config).\n\nExample\n\n# Generate 800x600 pattern: discs from 12 px across to 20% of the short side\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, 0.2short_side), cutoff=2.0, seed=0x1234)\n\n\n\n\n\nblob_pattern(pattern_resolution::Size2; kwargs...)\n\nGenerate a blob pattern with specified resolution.\n\nArguments\n\npattern_resolution: Pattern size as Size2 with pixel or logical units\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nblob_sizes: disc diameter range (see the pixel_type form)\nOther arguments same as blob_pattern(pixel_type, pattern_resolution; kwargs...), except the pixel type: it is positional, blob_pattern(T, pattern_resolution) (a pixel_type keyword is an ArgumentError; the default is DEFAULT_PIXEL_TYPE)\n\nReturns (pattern, blobs, config).\n\nExample\n\nusing VisualGeometryCore: Size2, px\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(Size2(width=800px, height=600px); blob_sizes=(12px, 120px), cutoff=2.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.blob_pattern"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.short_side","page":"Public API","text":"short_side\n\nMarker for a size relative to the pattern's short side: 0.2short_side is 20% of it. Valid as the MAX of blob_sizes, a disc diameter like any other size (blob_sizes = (2mm, 0.2short_side) makes the largest disc's diameter 20% of the short side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.short_side"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.ShortSideFraction","page":"Public API","text":"ShortSideFraction(fraction)\n\nA size given as a fraction of the pattern's short side — what fraction * short_side builds. Resolved to px once the pattern size is known; never stored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.ShortSideFraction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.calc_blob_scales","page":"Public API","text":"calc_blob_scales(min_scale, max_scale; scales_per_octave = 3) -> Vector\n\nThe blob-scale ladder from min_scale to max_scale, both exact: k + 1 scales, k the whole number of 1/scales_per_octave-octave steps nearest the span, spaced evenly in log. When max_scale lies on the 2^(1/scales_per_octave) ladder the scales are exactly min_scale · 2^(i / scales_per_octave). Unitless px.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.calc_blob_scales"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.blob_board","page":"Public API","text":"blob_board(paper_size::String, print_density; kwargs...) -> BoardBundle\nblob_board(width, height, print_density; kwargs...) -> BoardBundle\nblob_board(canvas_size::Size2, print_density; kwargs...) -> BoardBundle\nblob_board(pixel_type, paper_size | width, height | canvas_size, print_density; kwargs...)\nblob_board(json_config::String; dir, format, pdf_standard, icc_profile, id_label) -> BoardBundle\nblob_board(preamble::BoardPreamble, board_sizes::LogicalBoardSizes;\n           dir, format, pdf_standard, icc_profile, id_label) -> BoardBundle\n\nGenerate a printable calibration board (blob pattern, ruler frame, QR code and uid corners) at an exact physical size, and optionally write the print file and its JSON.\n\nSizes are physical (mm, inch) and print_density is a density (300dpi); the pattern is rasterized at that density, one pattern pixel per printer dot. Requires using CairoMakie (it loads BlobBoardsAuthoringExt); format = \"tiff\" also needs using Libtiff_jll.\n\nArguments\n\npaper_size: a key of BlobBoards.PRINT_SIZES — \"A0\"…\"A5\", \"ANSI A\"…\"ANSI E1\", \"Letter\", \"US Letter\", \"Legal\", \"Tabloid\", \"Ledger\" (portrait, except \"Ledger\"). Anything else is an ArgumentError.\nwidth, height or canvas_size = Size2(width = …, height = …): the page (canvas) size, physical lengths.\nprint_density: the print resolution, e.g. 300dpi.\npixel_type (optional, leading): the pattern's pixel type, e.g. Gray{N0f16} (default Gray{N0f8}). It is positional only — pixel_type = … as a keyword is an ArgumentError.\njson_config: the text of a board JSON sidecar; re-generates that board bit-exactly from its stored configuration (placement is re-run from the seed), under its stored identity (hashes.config: same uid and file name). The output keywords default to the JSON's render block, how the sheet was printed (a file without one: PDF/X-4, no id label); a keyword overrides it. The sidecar is written in the current schema, and the QR encodes the same preamble.\npreamble, board_sizes: the low-level form the others reduce to — a complete BlobBoards.BoardPreamble and its BlobBoards.LogicalBoardSizes.\n\nKeywords\n\nThe paper-size, width/height and Size2 forms (and their pixel_type variants) take every keyword below; the JSON and preamble forms take the Output group only. A keyword outside this list is an ArgumentError, never silently ignored.\n\nOutput (validated before placement)\n\ndir = nothing: output directory, created if missing. Writes blob_board_<uid>.<format> and blob_board_<uid>.json (the sidecar that reproduces the board), overwriting. nothing writes no file and only returns the bundle; giving format, pdf_standard, icc_profile or id_label without a dir is then an ArgumentError. The JSON also records, outside the board's identity, how the file was rendered (its render block).\nformat = \"pdf\": \"pdf\" — the print master (vector frame, pattern embedded pixel for pixel, lossless, rewritten by Ghostscript to pdf_standard; PDF/X-4 needs Ghostscript ≥ 10.07); \"tiff\" (or \"tif\") — the whole sheet at exactly print_density, 8-bit grey, LZW, density in the resolution tags and the ICC profile embedded (needs using Libtiff_jll); \"png\" — single-channel grey, linear gamma, for digital use. Case-insensitive; anything else is an ArgumentError.\npdf_standard = :x4: :x4 (PDF/X-4, what most print shops ask for), :x3, :x1a (PDF 1.3, for older RIPs) or :none (a plain lossless PDF with no OutputIntent). \"pdf\" only: giving it with another format is an ArgumentError.\nicc_profile = nothing: the grey profile declaring how the values print — nothing or :linear (linear reflectance, the default) or the path of a single-channel ICC file. Used as the PDF/X OutputIntent and embedded in a TIFF (which embeds the linear profile by default); giving it with \"png\" or with pdf_standard = :none is an ArgumentError.\nid_label = true: print the board's identity (file stem, full config hash, BlobBoards version, size @ dpi) in one 6 pt line under the board, in the sheet margin outside the TrimBox. Skipped when the margin has no room (with a warning for a frameless board, which then carries no printed identity). A rendering option, not part of the identity.\n\nPattern (recorded in the board's PatternConfig; see blob_pattern)\n\nblob_sizes = (4.0mm, nothing): the smallest and largest printed disc DIAMETER (2·cutoff·σ), physical lengths converted through print_density. max may be a length, a fraction of the pattern's short side (0.2short_side) or nothing (20% of the short side). The min may not be nothing or a fraction; its default is 2·cutoff·1mm (4 mm at the default cutoff). Both ends are exact. The smallest disc must be at least 2·cutoff·2 printer dots (σ ≥ 2 px: 0.68 mm at 300 dpi), reported in mm when violated, and the largest must be smaller than the pattern's short side. Stored as σ in px (min_scale, max_scale; those names are not keywords).\nseed: the placement RNG seed (any integer; default a random UInt64, recorded in the bundle). Same seed and keywords → the same board.\npolarity = :dark: :dark blobs on white or :light blobs on black.\nscales_per_octave = 3: rungs per octave of the blob-scale ladder.\nα = 1.2: scale-sampling bias (how many blobs each scale gets).\ncutoff = 2.0 (DEFAULT_CUTOFF): the disc radius in σ; keep the default.\nmax_trials = 5000: placement attempts per blob before a scale gives up.\n\nA board's blob centres always sit on pixel centres (raster_aligned is recorded as true in its PatternConfig and is not a keyword).\n\nBoard, frame and rulers (recorded in the board's BoardConfig)\n\nboard_size = canvas: the board area, a Size2 of lengths ≤ the canvas in both dimensions, centred on the page with white margins; the PDF TrimBox marks it.\nframe_width = 0.075 · max(width, height): the frame band around the pattern; its square corners hold the QR code (top left; omitted with a warning when the encoded preamble does not fit the corner at this density) and the three uid characters. The pattern is board_size − 2·frame_width. 0mm gives a frameless board: the pattern fills the board, with no rulers, QR code or uid.\nframe_style = :ruler: what fills the frame — :ruler (tick marks), :saddle (a two-row checkerboard; the bundle then carries its saddle points) or :none (blank, corners only).\nframe_padding = 0mm: white inset between the frame's content (ticks, saddle squares, QR code, uid glyphs) and its edges; must be < frame_width / 2 unless frame_style = :none.\nmajor_tick_spacing = 10mm, minor_tick_spacing = 5mm: ruler tick intervals. The ticks are centred along each side; check the printed scale against them.\nmajor_tick_width = frame_width − 2·frame_padding, minor_tick_width = major_tick_width / 2: each tick's full drawn length across the frame band (physical units, centred in the band). The defaults fill the padded band with major ticks and draw minor ticks half as long. Requires 0 < minor_tick_width ≤ major_tick_width ≤ frame_width − 2·frame_padding, else an ArgumentError.\n\nNever keywords: canvas_size, print_density, pattern_resolution (derived from the positional arguments), min_scale, max_scale (use blob_sizes), pixel_type (positional), raster_aligned, blob_measure (sizes are always diameters) and ruler_width (removed: set the tick widths) are each an ArgumentError.\n\nReturns\n\nA BlobBoards.BoardBundle:\n\nbundle.preamble: the BoardConfig (board_config(bundle)) and PatternConfig (pattern_config(bundle)) — everything needed to re-generate the board; its hash is the uid printed in the corners.\nbundle.blobs: the placed blobs (Discs, px, pattern frame); use blobs(bundle, frame; units) for :board, :canvas or :world coordinates, or mm.\nbundle.saddle_points: the frame's saddle points (frame_style = :saddle only; empty otherwise), via saddle_points.\n\nExamples\n\nusing BlobBoards, CairoMakie\n\n# An A4 board at 300 dpi, written as PDF/X-4 + JSON\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; seed = 0xBEEF, dir = \"./out\")\n\n# A custom page, a 180 mm square board on it, 2–12 mm printed discs\nbundle = blob_board(200mm, 250mm, 600dpi;\n    board_size = Size2(width = 180mm, height = 180mm),\n    blob_sizes = (2mm, 12mm),\n    frame_style = :saddle, frame_padding = 1mm, dir = \"./out\")\n\n# 16-bit pattern, TIFF print master for a photo lab\nusing Libtiff_jll\nbundle = blob_board(Gray{N0f16}, \"A3\", 1200dpi; format = \"tiff\", dir = \"./out\")\n\n# Re-generate a stored board\nbundle = blob_board(read(\"./out/blob_board_abc_12345.json\", String); dir = \"./out2\")\n\nSee also\n\nblob_pattern (the pattern alone, in pixels), PatternConfig, BoardConfig, BlobBoards.PRINT_SIZES. BlobBoards.render_board re-renders a stored bundle without re-running placement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.blob_board"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.blobs","page":"Public API","text":"blobs(pb::PatternBundle) -> Vector{Disc}\n\nGet blobs from a pattern bundle in logical units (pixels, COLMAP convention).\n\nBlobs are stored and returned in COLMAP convention (pixel centers at 0.5, 0.5). Use EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia}) to convert to other conventions if needed.\n\nReturns\n\nVector of Disc objects in logical units (pixels)\n\nExample\n\nb = blobs(pattern_bundle)\n\n# Convert to Julia convention if needed for array indexing\n# (Disc is a primitive, so wrap the frame map's offset in a Translation —\n# EuclideanMap has no primitive-action methods yet; VGC 0.7.1 candidate.)\nb_julia = Translation(EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia}).t).(b)\n\n\n\n\n\nblobs(bundle::BoardBundle, reference_frame::Symbol; units=px) -> Vector{Disc}\n\nGet blobs from a board bundle with specified coordinate origin and units.\n\nCoordinates are always returned in COLMAP convention (pixel centers at 0.5, 0.5). Use EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia}) to convert to other conventions if needed.\n\nArguments\n\nbundle::BoardBundle: The board bundle containing blobs\nreference_frame::Symbol: Coordinate system origin\n:pattern, :board, :canvas - 2D image frames (y-down)\n:world - 3D world frame (centered, y-up, unitless mm; ignores units parameter)\nunits: Target unit for coordinates (default: px)\nLogical: px - pixel units\nPhysical: mm, cm, inch, etc. - length units\n\nExamples\n\n# Logical units (pixels) in canvas frame\nb = blobs(bundle, :canvas)\n\n# Physical units in pattern frame\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern; units=mm)\n\n# World coordinates for 3D/P3P (centered, y-up, unitless mm)\nb = blobs(bundle, :world)\nX = Point3.(vcat.(centroid.(b), 0.0))  # 3D points on z=0 plane\n\n# Compose transforms for full pipeline\nT = EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia}) ∘ Translation(board_config(bundle), :pattern, :canvas)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.blobs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.saddle_points","page":"Public API","text":"saddle_points(bundle::BoardBundle, reference_frame::Symbol; units=px) -> Vector{Point2}\n\nGet saddle points from a board bundle with specified coordinate origin and units.\n\nSaddle points are at pixel boundaries (integer coordinates in COLMAP convention). Use EuclideanMap(PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:julia}) to convert to other conventions if needed.\n\nArguments\n\nbundle::BoardBundle: The board bundle containing saddle points\nreference_frame::Symbol: Coordinate system origin (:pattern, :board, or :canvas)\nunits: Target unit for coordinates (default: px)\nLogical: px - returns points with pixel units\nPhysical: mm, cm, inch, etc. - returns scaled unitless points\n\nExamples\n\n# Logical units in canvas frame\npts = saddle_points(bundle, :canvas)\n\n# Physical units in pattern frame\npts = saddle_points(bundle, :pattern; units=mm)\npts = saddle_points(bundle, :pattern; units=inch)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.saddle_points"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.find_boards","page":"Public API","text":"find_boards(board_feature_dict, descriptor_network, img::AbstractMatrix;\n            board_bundles, config = FindBoardsConfig(), device = default_device(),\n            gpu_index = nothing, matching_strategy = MutualNN(),\n            exclude_polys = nothing, telemetry = nothing) -> Vector\nfind_boards(board_feature_dict, descriptor_network, frames; kwargs...) -> Vector{Vector}\nfind_boards(board_feature_dict, image_features; kwargs...) -> Vector\n\nFind known boards in an image and estimate each one's homography or pose; returns one (uid, model, quality, scored) tuple per verified board.\n\nThe image method runs the whole pipeline once per image: detect blobs, adapt them to ellipses, canonicalize and describe them, then for every board match, fit (RANSAC + local optimization), gate on inlier count and appearance, and claim the board's image region so that no detection serves two boards.\n\nArguments\n\nboard_feature_dict: Dict(uid => features) of the gallery boards' descriptors, as returned by insert!(::BoardGallery, board_paths, network) (BoardGallery). Only these boards are searched for.\ndescriptor_network: a DescriptorNetwork (or the path of its ONNX file), the same network that described the gallery.\nimg: a grey image (AbstractMatrix, e.g. Gray.(load(path))). Give a vector of frames to run each independently with the gallery staged once. Give features from extract_image_features to skip the image stages.\n\nKeywords\n\nboard_bundles (required): Dict(uid => BoardBundle) for every board in board_feature_dict (load_board(path).bundle). Polarity, cutoff and the board outline used for spatial claiming come from them.\nconfig = FindBoardsConfig(): every pipeline setting (FindBoardsConfig). config.geometry selects the estimator: HomographyGeometry (no camera), PoseGeometry(camera) (calibrated P3P) or RadialHomographyGeometry(image_size) (unknown radially distorted camera). Build it from a TOML file with FindBoardsConfig(path; camera).\ndevice = default_device(): the KernelAbstractions backend for detection, adaptation and description — KA.CPU(), or CUDABackend() once CUDA is loaded (the default picks the GPU when one is functional).\ngpu_index = nothing: a prebuilt GalleryIndex of the gallery on device; built per call when nothing. Build it once to reuse across images.\nmatching_strategy = MutualNN(): the first matching phase's nearest-neighbour rule (a permissive KnnMatch(3) cleanup phase follows).\nexclude_polys = nothing: image polygons whose detections are excluded before matching (regions already claimed elsewhere).\ntelemetry = nothing: a Telemetry sink that records per-stage diagnostics.\n\nReturns\n\nA vector with one (uid, model, quality, scored) tuple per accepted board, in acceptance order:\n\nuid: the board's 3-character uid (the corner glyphs, a key of board_bundles).\nmodel: with HomographyGeometry, the homography mapping the board's :world plane (mm, z = 0) to image pixels. With PoseGeometry, the board → camera pose (model.R, model.t in mm). With RadialHomographyGeometry, the (H, k) division-model fit.\nquality: the fit's RANSAC score.\nscored: the correspondences (board blob → image detection) with their posterior weights in scored.weights; count(is_map_inlier, scored.weights) is the inlier count.\n\nSee also\n\ntrack_boards (video), FindBoardsConfig, BoardGallery, DescriptorNetwork.\n\n\n\n\n\nfind_boards(board_feature_dict, descriptor_network,\n            frames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}; kwargs...) -> Vector{Vector}\n\nBatch entry point: run board detection on a SEQUENCE of frames with one-time setup amortization — the gallery GPU index (and each frame's detection plan) is built once and reused. Per-frame results are independent (no temporal association — see track_boards for that); element i is exactly what find_boards(..., frames[i]) returns.\n\n\n\n\n\nfind_boards(board_feature_dict, image_features::StructArray{<:DescribedFeature};\n            kwargs...) -> Vector\n\nMatch pre-extracted image features against all boards, estimate geometry, gate on inlier count + appearance, and spatially claim/suppress. Runs round-based spatial suppression: every round fits all live boards on the current claim state, commits the single best, removes it from the gallery, and claims the detections inside its projected quad; surviving boards re-resolve their matches against the new claim state and refit, until a round accepts nothing. Two matching phases run this loop — strict MutualNN, then a permissive KnnMatch(3) cleanup on the depleted pool.\n\nArguments\n\nboard_feature_dict: Dict{uid => StructArray{DescribedFeature}} of board features\nimage_features: Pre-extracted StructArray{DescribedFeature} from extract_image_features\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nconfig: FindBoardsConfig. config.geometry selects the path — HomographyGeometry() (no camera) or PoseGeometry(camera) (calibrated P3P).\nboard_bundles: Dict{uid => BoardBundle} — required for spatial claiming and guided search (enforced with an ArgumentError).\ndevice: KA device (KA.CPU() or CUDABackend())\ngpu_index: Pre-built GalleryIndex for the device-resident board gallery\n\nReturns (uid, model, quality, scored) for each verified board (IRLS weights in scored.weights), concatenated across phases.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.find_boards"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.track_boards","page":"Public API","text":"track_boards(board_feature_dict, descriptor_network, video_path::AbstractString; kwargs...)\n\nTrack boards through a video file. Opens video_path and streams it frame by frame — nothing is materialized, since the sequence method only ever enumerates its input.\n\nThis mirrors annotate_blobs and save_trace, which already take video paths directly; VideoIO is a hard dependency, so accepting one here costs nothing and saves every caller an openvideo + close dance.\n\n\n\n\n\ntrack_boards(board_feature_dict, descriptor_network, images;\n             board_bundles, config=FindBoardsConfig(),\n             iou_threshold=config.track.iou_threshold,\n             support_fraction=config.track.support_fraction,\n             patience=config.track.patience,\n             keyframe_every=config.track.keyframe_every, device=default_device(),\n             gpu_index=nothing, on_frame=nothing)\n    -> Dict{String, Vector{BoardDetection}}\n\nTrack boards across a sequence of images: motion-guided association plus a per-frame RANSAC over the associated correspondences (see the file header for the algorithm). Calibration (if any) lives in config.geometry.\n\niou_threshold: per-blob predicted↔detected ellipse IoU gate ([find_boards.track]; tolerates roughly ½σ of center prediction error but rejects wrong-octave scale).\nsupport_fraction: fraction of predicted blobs that must associate for the warm bet to proceed to estimation.\npatience: consecutive failed frames before a track stops being re-targeted every frame (it stays eligible for discovery keyframes).\nkeyframe_every: discovery cadence for boards not currently tracked (0 disables periodic discovery after frame 1). NB between keyframes only boards ALREADY tracked are searched for, so a board first appearing at frame k is not looked for until the next keyframe — correct for video, and the reason unrelated stills belong in find_boards instead.\ngpu_index: prebuilt GalleryIndex, as on find_boards. Built here when omitted; supply one to reuse the staged gallery across several media instead of re-uploading it per call. When supplied, the INDEX defines which boards are matched — an index from a different gallery matches the wrong boards silently.\non_frame: a sink called on_frame(frame::TrackFrame) after every frame with that frame's record, pixels and configuration of record (a failing sink warns and tracking continues). Capture a replayable trace with trace = TrackTrace(); track_boards(…; on_frame = f -> push!(trace, f)).\n\nReturns Dict{uid => Vector{BoardDetection}}; each detection's source records whether the frame was won by the :track association path, a targeted :init, or :detect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.track_boards"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.FindBoardsConfig","page":"Public API","text":"FindBoardsConfig(; kwargs...)\n\nConfiguration for find_boards pipeline. The geometry field (HomographyGeometry or PoseGeometry) groups the per-estimator knobs (outlier half-width, min inliers) and determines which path runs.\n\nDetection\n\ndetect::DetectConfig = DetectConfig(): Detection configuration (single source of truth for detect_blobs parameters across all backends).\nmax_detections=typemax(Int): post-detection response-rank cap. On the grouped image path it is applied PER POLARITY GROUP (after _polarity_subset), so a mixed dark+light board set can process up to 2× this many detections in total — see _detect_for_boards for why the shared detection stage must stay uncapped. Single-polarity sets (every real set) behave exactly like a detect_blobs(; max_detections=…) cap.\n\nAdaptation (blob fitting)\n\nadapt::AdaptConfig = AdaptConfig(): Adaptation configuration\n\nGeometry estimator\n\ngeometry::GeometryConfig = HomographyGeometry(): per-estimator RANSAC + inlier-gate knobs. Use HomographyGeometry() for the no-camera path or PoseGeometry(camera) (the camera lives in the geometry) for the calibrated P3P path.\n\nMatching\n\nmin_similarity=0.6: Minimum descriptor cosine similarity. The derived gates move with it: phase 2's permissive gate (_phase2_similarity, 0.1 below), which is not configured separately so it cannot drift apart under a sweep. The agenda counts every resolved match — there is no second similarity bar (see _n_resolved_matches).\n\nAppearance verification (mandatory)\n\nverify_similarity=0.6: an inlier correspondence \"agrees in appearance\" when its gallery and image descriptors reach this cosine similarity. Deliberately NOT named min_similarity — a different quantity at a different stage that happens to share the value (see appearance_support in verify.jl).\nmin_appearance_support=0.5: minimum fraction of a candidate board's inliers that must agree in appearance for the board to be accepted.\n\nGeometry cannot make this call. Guided association is descriptor-blind, and at measured detection densities (13,844/MP, 5 px band) 66% of arbitrary positions have SOME detection in range — a fabricated board fills its inlier set geometrically, so counts and fractions satisfy it. The inliers' APPEARANCE separates real from fabricated with zero measured overlap (real ≥ 0.993 support, false = 0.0 at a 0.8 sim gate; see verify.jl). This gate replaced the geometry structs' min_inlier_fraction (a proxy for the same attack, removed 2026-08-13).\n\nverify_similarity was calibrated DOWN twice on the 260527 corpus (2026-08-14), each time because the real population's frontier sat under the gate while false boards never came near it (median 0.07–0.37):   0.8 → 0.7  on the strided 12-frame slice — distant real boards carry              median inlier sims 0.73–0.80; 0.8 rejected 8 GT-verified              real boards.   0.7 → 0.6  on the FULL corpus — the inter-slice 2–3 m frames the stride              never sampled dip to median sims 0.66–0.71, so 0.7 gated 13              more real csponds (probe: support 0.30–0.47, deterministic). At the default support of 0.5 the gate is exactly a MEDIAN test, so the bar must sit under the real population's frontier median. NB appearance is structurally blind to POSE FLIPS (correct correspondences, mirrored rotation): that is resolve_pose_ambiguity's job, not this gate's — and loosening this bar re-admits flipped poses along with good ones.\n\nReproducibility\n\nransac_seed=0x0b10b5eed: base seed for the per-fit RANSAC streams (Xoshiro(ransac_seed ⊻ board uid) — see fit_rng). Identical inputs give identical results, independent of thread scheduling, call order, and ambient RNG state; sweep it only to MEASURE fit-stage RNG sensitivity. CUDA feature extraction remains nondeterministic (adapt's atomic pixel slots), so end-to-end reproducibility is \"given features\".\n\nEvery field is CONCRETE by design: this config is read inside the per-board fitting loop, so an abstract field (adapt::AdaptConfig, geometry) would force boxing and dynamic dispatch on every access. Keep it that way when adding fields.\n\nOverriding one knob\n\nThe struct is immutable and find_boards has no per-knob keyword — you supply a whole config. To change one setting, use Accessors.@set, which reaches nested fields and rebuilds only what changed:\n\nAccessors is NOT a dependency of BlobBoards — add it to your own project (pkg> add Accessors). No library code is needed for this to work: ConstructionBase's generic setproperties already handles these structs.\n\nusing Accessors                       # your dep, not BlobBoards'\ncfg = FindBoardsConfig(\"configs/default.toml\"; camera)\ncfg = @set cfg.detect.peak_threshold = 0.02\ncfg = @set cfg.detect.upsample = false\n\nThis works through ConstructionBase's generic setproperties, which is correct here only because every one of these structs is a plain @kwdef with no inner constructor — so its positional constructor matches its field order. Adding a validating inner constructor to FindBoardsConfig, DetectConfig, AdaptConfig or a GeometryConfig silently breaks the override idiom; test/test_config_toml.jl gates that, including the type-changing case (@set cfg.detect.nms = EllipseNms(...), which alters a type parameter).\n\nSweeps should override rather than re-list: a hand-built FindBoardsConfig in a driver duplicates the knobs of the config of record, and the two drift. See bmvc2026/scripts/tune_invariance.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.FindBoardsConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.PoseGeometry","page":"Public API","text":"PoseGeometry(camera; outlier_halfwidth=5.0, min_inliers=6, local_optimization=StudentTUniformIrls())\n\nCalibrated P3P estimator config. Holds the camera (a CameraModel carrying the lens/distortion model used to undistort the image). The camera lives here — not as a separate find_boards argument — so calibrated-ness is encoded in one place and illegal camera/geometry mismatches are unrepresentable.\n\nundistortion selects where the pose is measured: :none (the default) detects, adapts and describes on the RAW image and the estimator verifies through the FULL camera — RANSAC scoring, the Student-t M-step and the Welsch lift all measure the reprojection through the lens in the pixels the detector produced (RobustVisualGeometry ≥ 0.19.9), and guided association transfers each blob's conic through the plane homography exactly and the lens's affinity at its centre (transfer_ellipse(::Camera, _)). :global undistorts the IMAGE (VGC's fused warp) and runs the whole pipeline on the result through the pinhole twin — the historical path, and what the BMVC runs used (their configs state it). Why :none: a residual in the undistorted plane is the detection error magnified by the lens's inverse slope — 84× at the rim of an 8 mm fisheye — so a 5 px gate there rejected every rim point; in raw pixels the gate means what it says everywhere.\n\n3 is the geometric minimum for p3p; the default 6 leaves three outlier-tolerated points above the minimal sample. P3P is 2 DOF tighter than homography, so it needs one fewer support point than the homography path (mininliers=7). (The gate is INCLUSIVE — `nin >= mininliers` — since 2026-08-11; the default went 5 → 6 in the same change, keeping the effective tuned threshold: a 5-inlier P3P fit against a 50-board gallery measured as a false accept on DSC02847.) Half-width is 5.0 px — the same inlier band as the homography path — since BB residuals come from adapt-refined sub-pixel detections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.PoseGeometry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.HomographyGeometry","page":"Public API","text":"HomographyGeometry(; outlier_halfwidth=5.0, min_inliers=7, local_optimization=StudentTUniformIrls())\n\nHomography (no-camera) estimator config. 4 is the geometric minimum for find_homography; the default 7 leaves three outlier-tolerated points above the minimal sample — homography is 2 DOF looser than P3P, so it needs more support to reject coincidental 4-point matches against a large board gallery. (The gate is INCLUSIVE — nin >= min_inliers — since 2026-08-11; the default went 6 → 7 in the same change, so the effective threshold the pipeline was tuned under is unchanged.) Half-width is tighter than RVG's default (20) since BB residuals come from adapt-refined sub-pixel detections.\n\nThere is no min_inlier_fraction anymore. It gated on the fraction of the correspondence set with posterior weight above ½ (is_map_inlier) — a geometric proxy against dense low-quality match clouds (guided sets of 400+ csponds carrying 6 accidental inliers satisfy any absolute count). The mandatory appearance gate (FindBoardsConfig.verify_similarity / min_appearance_support, see verify.jl) rejects that attack directly and with total measured separation, so the proxy was removed (2026-08-13) rather than kept as a second, weaker knob on the same failure mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.HomographyGeometry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.RadialHomographyGeometry","page":"Public API","text":"RadialHomographyGeometry(image_size; outlier_halfwidth=5.0, min_inliers=7, local_optimization=StudentTUniformIrls())\n\nUncalibrated radially distorted homography config — the one-sided division-model (H, k) path (RVG find_radial_homography; Wadenbäck et al., 3DV 2026). For captures with an UNKNOWN camera whose lens bends straight lines: the distortion coefficient is estimated jointly with H per fit — no calibration, no image undistortion (the detector runs on the raw image, exactly like the plain homography arm).\n\nimage_size = (width, height) is DATA, not configuration — the sensor extent that fixes the distortion centre (the image middle), which the arm hands its estimator as center — so like PoseGeometry's camera it lives in the geometry and arrives as a FindBoardsConfig keyword, never as a TOML value.\n\nmin_inliers defaults to the homography arm's 7: the model has one extra DOF, and the count gate stays inclusive (nin >= min_inliers).\n\nv1 scope: the guided final fit and track_boards are NOT wired for this arm (both project through a single HomographyMap, which cannot express the distortion); the RANSAC+LO fit is the result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.RadialHomographyGeometry"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.prepare_image_features","page":"Public API","text":"prepare_image_features(descriptor_network, img;\n    board_bundles, uids = keys(board_bundles), config::FindBoardsConfig, device)\n    -> (; detect_img, adapt_img, image_features, shared_cutoff, config)\n\nThe camera (for the BrownConrady undistort) is read from config.geometry — PoseGeometry carries it, HomographyGeometry has none (undistort becomes a no-op).\n\nDetect-once seam for annotate_blobs, track_boards and the sweeps: the SAME stages find_boards(…, img) runs — undistort (two-step: a KA undistort kernel for BrownConrady, then a storage conversion via an identity-kernel _undistort_adapt), detect at the polarity the boards imply, cap, printed- profile scale correction, adapt, describe at the gallery's shared extent (_shared_image_cutoff over uids) — producing ONE feature set shared by every board. There is no cutoff keyword: the gallery decides the extent, and a caller-supplied one could only make query and gallery descriptors silently incomparable. The returned config carries the derived detection polarity; shared_cutoff is the extent the features were described at.\n\nNEVER run two undistort kernels on the same image — the per-stage numerical drift between kernels (hardware bilinear vs manual bilinear) propagates through adapt → canonicalize → descriptors and shifts RANSAC inliers enough to drop borderline boards. The second _undistort_adapt(…, nothing) call here is a storage convert (Pinhole/nothing path), not a second kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.prepare_image_features"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.load_board","page":"Public API","text":"load_board(board_path) -> NamedTuple\n\nParse board JSON metadata: extract UID, GT blob positions, and world coordinates. No PDF rasterization — just metadata.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple with:\n\nuid::String — unique board identifier (SHA-256 hash)\nbundle — parsed BoardBundle\nconfig_hash::String — the full SHA-256 the uid abbreviates (a caller that needs both no longer re-reads the bundle for it)\nimage_path::String — path to the image/PDF (for deferred rasterization)\ncanvas_frames::Vector{Detection} — GT blob detections in canvas pixel coordinates\nworld_frames::Vector{Detection} — GT blobs in world coordinates (mm, unitless)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.load_board"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.load_bundle","page":"Public API","text":"load_bundle(path) -> (bundle, image_path, config_hash)\n\nParse the JSON sidecar to get the typed bundle (BoardBundle or PatternBundle), resolve the image path, and return the stored configuration hash.\n\nThe config_hash is the hex-encoded SHA-256 hash stored at write time in the hashes.config field of the JSON.  This is the authoritative board identity — use uid_from_hash(config_hash) to derive the 3-character UID rather than recomputing from the deserialized config (which may differ after format migrations).\n\nSee also: load for loading bundle + image together.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.load_bundle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.GalleryIndex","page":"Public API","text":"GalleryIndex(device, board_feature_dict)\n\nThe board gallery staged on device as ONE block.\n\nThe board set is one gallery, so there is one staging and one matching path (see match_boards). stacked is the host half (StackedGallery): the concatenated features and the prefix sums that map a row of the block back to (board, local blob). stacked_index is the device half, VGF's StackedDescriptorIndex over those same descriptors. Built once per find_boards call and threaded as gpu_index.\n\nIt used to stage the descriptors TWICE, adding a per-board blocking for KnnMatch. That was ~85 MB of duplicate device memory for nothing: in VGF's stacked matcher the blocking (offsets/sizes) reaches exactly one kernel, the REVERSE reduction _ka_top1_cols_blocked!. The forward reduction runs per gallery row over all features, and KnnMatch's _ka_top4_rows! likewise — \"the top-4 reduction is per row and there is no column reduction to keep block-local\". So only MutualNN can see the blocking at all, and for it the per-board answer is the wrong question (below).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.GalleryIndex"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.detect_blobs","page":"Public API","text":"detect_blobs(img; config=DetectConfig(),\n             max_detections=typemax(Int)) -> Vector{Detection}\n\nDetect blobs in an image via the KernelAbstractions ASIFT pipeline, then apply a BB-side response-rank cap.\n\nReturns raw detections. Use adapt for shape fitting and filter_blobs for quality filtering.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nconfig::DetectConfig: detector-stage parameters (see DetectConfig). These are read by the detector backend.\nmax_detections=typemax(Int): Final cap by response magnitude. Default is effectively no cap; supply a finite integer to enable.\n\nmax_detections is a BB-side post-processing knob, not an input the detector backend consumes — that's why it lives here rather than on DetectConfig.\n\nThere is deliberately no cutoff: detection is scale-space extrema and is cutoff-INDEPENDENT. The kwarg existed \"for API parity\" and was a documented no-op, which made cutoff look like a detection parameter when it is actually a property of the BOARD, consumed downstream by adapt, filter_blobs and canonicalize. That misreading is what let a run config state a cutoff that disagreed with the boards.\n\nCross-warp NMS (ellipse-aware) is configured through config.nms (MxmNms / EllipseNms); there is no BB-side geometric NMS stage over the detector's elliptic output (the legacy suppress_overlapping path only knows Disc bbox IoU and is bypassed).\n\nExample\n\ndetections = detect_blobs(img)\nframes = adapt(detections, img; cutoff=2.0)\nframes = filter_blobs(frames, detections; cutoff=2.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.detect_blobs"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.DetectConfig","page":"Public API","text":"DetectConfig(; kwargs...)\n\nSingle source of truth for detect_blobs parameters across all backends (KA.CPU(), CUDABackend(), …). Each device implementation reads the fields it understands and ignores the rest; ASIFT-specific fields are no-ops for non-ASIFT backends.\n\nDefaults match examples/pipeline/find_boards.jl — the canonical tuned invocation.\n\nGeneric fields (all backends)\n\npeak_threshold = 0.01: DoG/intensity response threshold\nedge_threshold = 5.0: edge-rejection ratio\npolarity = :dark: which polarity to keep (:dark, :light, or :none for the unfiltered superset). NB :none — VGF throws on :both.\n\nThe per-call detection cap (max_detections) lives as a top-level kwarg of detect_blobs, not here — it's a BB-side post-NMS cap on the returned vector, not something the detector device consumes.\n\nASIFT-specific (no-ops for non-ASIFT backends)\n\ntilts = []: ASIFT tilt schedule; EMPTY means the IMAS-25 schedule (the same convention configs/default.toml uses)\nnms = EllipseNms(cutoff = 2.0f0): cross-warp NMS strategy (MxmNms(), EllipseNms(), or nothing to disable and let downstream RANSAC handle the duplicates).\n\nParameterized on the NMS config type N (Nothing, MxmNms, or EllipseNms) so the field stays concrete rather than an abstract union.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.DetectConfig"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.adapt","page":"Public API","text":"adapt(detections, img; config=AdaptConfig(), device = default_device()) -> Vector{BlobDetection}\n\nFit elliptical frames to raw blob detections in img — the detections are the shapes being refined, so they lead. Both are typed, so a call in the retired (img, detections) order is a MethodError.\n\nUses a unified IRLS loop with Student-t posterior weights. The solve step is the Student-t/uniform mixture: WLS in log-space with f² correction and the sort-and-sweep posterior partition. It is not configurable.\n\nAccepts Vector{Detection} (from detect_blobs) or Vector{Disc} (ground truth).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{BlobDetection{Float64}} — fitted elliptical frames with .id indexing into the original detection vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.adapt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.AdaptConfig","page":"Public API","text":"AdaptConfig(; kwargs...)\n\nConfiguration for blob adaptation (paraboloid fitting).\n\nIRLS\n\nspatial_weighting::Bool = true — adaptive spatial weights from current shape\ncutoff::Float64 = 2.0 — pixel window in units of σ\nmax_irls_iter::Int = 20 — maximum IRLS iterations\nrtol::Float64 = 1e-6 — relative parameter convergence threshold\n\nFiltering\n\nmin_intensity::Float64 = 1e-6 — minimum inverted intensity to include pixel\nmax_scale_fraction::Float64 = 0.1 — skip detections whose σ exceeds this FRACTION of the image's √area, i.e. σ > f·√(W·H). A resource guard: a blob's fitted support is ~(2·cutoff·σ)² pixels, so an unbounded σ is an OOM. It is a fraction rather than a pixel count because the same absolute cap means different things on different sensors — 200 px was ~5.8% of √area at 12 MP and larger than the whole frame at VGA, so an absolute value could not transfer across cameras. √area (the side of the equivalent square) is used rather than min(W,H) or the diagonal so the cap is aspect-neutral.\nmax_scale_ratio::Float64 = 4.0 — reject if adapted σ / detection σ exceeds this\nscan_padding::Float64 = 1.0 — per-blob scan box halfwidth = cutoff · scan_padding · σ (decoupled from max_scale_ratio)\nmax_clip_ratio::Float64 = 0.2 — reject if blob is clipped by image boundary\nmax_aspect_ratio::Float64 = 10.0 — reject if ellipse axis ratio exceeds this\n\nAccumulation and the 6×6 solve run in Float64 wherever the backend supports it (Float32 on Metal); per-pixel staging is pinned Float32. (store was deleted 2026-09: inert since the KA port — no kernel read it.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.AdaptConfig"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.canonicalize","page":"Public API","text":"canonicalize(frames, img; mode=:logpolar, device=default_device(), kwargs...)\n    -> Vector{Matrix{Gray{Float32}}}\n\nExtract normalized patches of img at frames (Detection, EllipticFrame, Disc, etc.) — the frames are the shapes the patches are sampled on, so they lead. Both are typed, so a call in the retired (img, frames) order is a MethodError.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nmode=:logpolar: patch parameterization — :logpolar, :cartesian, or :original (see this file's header for the exact geometry of each)\npatch_size=64: Output patch size (square)\ninner_extent=2.0: Inner radius in units of σ (only used by :logpolar)\nouter_extent=DEFAULT_OUTER_EXTENT (96σ): outer radius in units of σ (field-of-view for all modes); gallery and query patches must share it\ndevice = default_device(): Computation device\nsupersample=1: Anti-aliasing supersample factor. When >1, the patch is sampled on a supersample× finer grid and box-averaged down to patch_size. At large outer_extent the log-polar (and cartesian/original) maps minify the source — one output texel spans many input pixels — so a single interpolated tap aliases; supersampling averages that footprint. Cost scales as supersample². (See out_debug/logpolar_aliasing_oe96.png.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.canonicalize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.describe","page":"Public API","text":"describe(model::DescriptorNetwork, patches) -> Matrix{Float32}\n\nRun ONNX descriptor model on patches in batched mode. Returns an N × D descriptor matrix (L2-normalized by the model).\n\nThe per-call batch is model.max_batch, set at construction — it is not a call-site keyword, because it also fixes the arena this network reserved (see reserve_workspace!). Measured on 3247 real 64×64 patches (RTX 4090, log-polar s96 network, 3 GiB arena cap), µs/patch by max_batch:\n\n32 → 61.7   64 → 63.1   128 → 76.8   256 → 91.7   512 → 442.4   0 → 472.0\n\nHence the default of 64. Read the right-hand columns with care: those runs predate the batch-derived cap, and batch 512 wants 4.1 GiB against the 3 GiB ceiling they were measured under, so part of that cliff is the arena thrashing rather than the convolutions. Batching is numerically exact (each patch is normalized independently; the graph has no BatchNorm), so this only ever trades throughput.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.describe"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.DescriptorNetwork","page":"Public API","text":"DescriptorNetwork <: AbstractDescriptorNetwork\n\nONNX-based descriptor model. Loads a pre-exported ONNX file for inference via ONNXRunTime.\n\nConstructors\n\nDescriptorNetwork(path::AbstractString) — load from ONNX file\n\nmax_batch is a FIELD rather than a describe keyword because it fixes the size of the CUDA arena this network reserves (reserve_workspace!). A reservation made for one batch size that a later call could exceed would not be a reservation at all.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.DescriptorNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.BoardGallery","page":"Public API","text":"BoardGallery\n\nPersistent store of board descriptors keyed by board UID.\n\nWraps a JLD2 file with extraction parameters baked into the cache key, so changing the model or extraction settings produces fresh entries. Each board's Gaussian cutoff is read from its bundle metadata.\n\nFields\n\npath::String — JLD2 file path\nnetwork_hash::String — SHA-256 of the ONNX descriptor model\npatch_size::Int — canonical patch size\nouter_extent::Float64 — outer sampling radius\n\nConstructor\n\ngallery = BoardGallery(\"boards.jld2\", \"model.onnx\")\ngallery = BoardGallery(\"boards.jld2\", model::AbstractDescriptorNetwork)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.BoardGallery"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.annotate_blobs","page":"Public API","text":"annotate_blobs(board_feature_dict, descriptor_network, img;\n               board_bundles, device = default_device(), kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\nDetect boards in img, project GT blobs into the image, and extract canonical patches labeled by (board_uid, blob_id).\n\nAlso extracts confuser patches: image detections that were not matched to any board blob. These are canonicalized using the same pipeline and returned separately for use as negatives during contrastive training.\n\nReturns (; board_uids, blob_ids, patches, confuser_patches).\n\n\n\n\n\nannotate_blobs(board_feature_dict, descriptor_network, video_path;\n               output_dir, board_bundles, board_paths, kwargs...)\n\nProcess video frames, detect boards, and write per-frame JLD2 files into output_dir. Reference patches from the board PDF are written to output_dir/refs/<uid>.jld2 on first detection.\n\nWrites output_dir/<video_basename>.done after all frames are processed. Resumable: existing per-frame files are skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.annotate_blobs"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.MxmNms","page":"Public API","text":"MxmNms(; window_half=3, band_octaves=1.0f0, band_reach=1)\n\nM×M centroid-window cross-warp NMS. Two ASIFT features collide if their centers fall inside an M = 2*window_half + 1 pixel box in input-frame coordinates; the lower-response one is dropped. Cheap; ignores ellipse shape. band_octaves/band_reach restrict suppression to features of comparable scale (see EllipseNms).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.MxmNms"},{"category":"type","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.EllipseNms","page":"Public API","text":"EllipseNms(; cutoff=2.0f0, band_octaves=1.0f0, band_reach=1)\n\nMahalanobis-footprint cross-warp NMS. Two ASIFT features collide if their cutoff-σ footprints share a pixel; the lower-response one is dropped. Respects ellipse shape. Suppression is SCALE-BANDED: a feature can only be suppressed by one whose geometric-mean scale band ⌊log2 √det S / band_octaves⌋ is within band_reach of its own (1 octave ± 1 ⇒ scale ratio < 4), so board-sized extrema and tilted-warp streaks cannot erase the small blobs inside them. band_octaves = 0 restores the whole-pool rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.EllipseNms"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.px_per_mm","page":"Public API","text":"px_per_mm(print_density) -> Float64\n\nConvert print density to pixels per millimeter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.px_per_mm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.mm_per_px","page":"Public API","text":"mm_per_px(print_density) -> VgcQuantity\n\nThe pixel pitch of a print density, as a mm/px quantity — the inverse of the density, converted (1 / 300dpi is 0.0847 mm/px). Until 2026-09 it returned a bare length in mm, dropping the px dimension its callers then had to supply by convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.mm_per_px"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.build_tracks_for_sequences","page":"Public API","text":"build_tracks_for_sequences(board_dir, descriptor_model_path, video_dir; kwargs...)\n    -> output_path\n\nBuild multi-modal training tracks for every media file in video_dir against every board .json in board_dir, accumulating into $output_name.tracks.\n\nVideos (.mp4, .mov, .avi, .mkv, .m4v) are sampled into frames; photos (.jpg, .jpeg, .png, .tif, .tiff, .bmp) are treated as a single frame. A photo yields at most one observation per tracked blob (paired against its GT anchor via the anchor sequence); n_frames/stride are ignored for photos.\n\nFor each media item the boards are detected + tracked across frames (track_boards), then blob correspondences are established per frame by transferring the board's GT blobs through the estimated world→image homography and validating by IoU. Each surviving track stores, per observation, a single patch view (patch_type) at a single scale, a per-pixel board∩in-frame mask, and the per-frame affine shape. Tracks hold only tracked observations; the GT anchor patches are not included.\n\nOne .tracks file therefore holds exactly one patch type at one scale — the output_name is auto-suffixed _<patch_type>_s<scale> so logpolar/cartesian runs (or different scales) never collide in the same file.\n\nEach patch carries a masks companion (same P×P×N shape): 1 where the pixel back-projects onto the board's world rectangle AND onto a real image pixel, 0 over off-board background, image edges, and perspective-warp gray-fill voids. Confuser (untracked) patches carry an in-frame-only mask (no board); anchor patches carry an in-frame mask against the rendered board image.\n\nTwo independent augmentations may fire per frame, both before extraction: a resolution-loss down/up-resample (probability p_downsample, coordinate- preserving) and a perspective warp (p_perspective) that strengthens the primary board's real foreshortening. The perspective warp composes its homography into every board's world→image map, so correspondences stay valid on the warped frame. A third, blur_sigma_max, adds Gaussian blur and is OFF by default.\n\nAdditionally, one anchor sequence per board is written, named by the board uid (rather than $(video)_$(uid)), holding every GT reference blob keyed by the same blob_track_id used by the per-video tracks — so each track's GT anchor is recoverable by id.\n\nBoards and their gallery features are keyed by uid (the board's SHA-256 hash, as produced by load_board) — the same key track_boards returns — so lookups stay consistent end to end.\n\nAlready-processed sequences present in an existing output file are skipped (both $(video)_$(uid) video sequences and $(uid) anchor sequences), so the call is resumable.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nn_frames / stride: frame sampling (mutually exclusive; default = every frame)\niou_threshold=0.6: minimum symmetric IoU for a valid blob correspondence. Kept fairly strict: a board's homography extrapolates its grid over nearby near-coplanar boards in a stack, and a loose threshold lets the dense fractal pattern produce coincidental cross-board false matches. 0.6 removes almost all of them (a handful survive per board vs hundreds of true matches) at a modest recall cost on fully-visible boards.\nmax_detections=typemax(Int): per-frame detection cap (set it, e.g. 5000, on high-resolution footage to keep the GPU descriptor-match GEMM in memory)\nmax_image_pixels=nothing: optional cap on each frame's total pixel count (aspect ratio preserved), e.g. 3840*2160 (≈8.3 MP). The default keeps the native resolution. The detector allocates a full image pyramid sized to the frame, so set this on high-res stills (a 24 MP phone photo). Applied at read time so detect/track/adapt/canonicalize all share one coordinate system.\nmin_inliers=20: board-acceptance threshold applied AFTER tracking — a tracked detection is kept only when its inlier count reaches this. It is a post-filter on track_boards' output, not a HomographyGeometry setting; the tracker itself runs the config-of-record gate (min_inliers = 7).\npatch_type=:logpolar: the single patch parameterization written to this file — :logpolar, :cartesian, or :original (see canonicalize).\nscale=32: the single patch scale — half the patch width/height in units of σ. Patches are stored P×P×N; patch_type/scale are recorded as attributes on each sequence's tracks group.\nconfuser_factor=1.0: cap total confusers (untracked patches) at confuser_factor × tracked patches, sampled at random. Each confuser stores the chosen patch view + its in-frame mask under untracked/{patches,masks}. Supply Inf to keep all confusers (no cap).\ndebug_dir=nothing: when set, save one PNG per processed frame into this directory — the frame the detector ran on, with every board detected in it overlaid in its own color: that board's matched GT blobs as projected ellipses plus its projected board rectangle. Colors are stable per board across frames and printed once at run start. Purely diagnostic; nothing disables it.\n\nTurning off the memory caps (large GPU)\n\nThree knobs bound GPU/host memory on constrained hardware; on a large GPU set them wide to process everything:\n\nmax_detections=typemax(Int) (default) — no per-frame detection cap. Lower it (e.g. 5000) only if the all-boards × all-detections match GEMM OOMs.\nmax_image_pixels=nothing — keep native frame resolution (the default).\nconfuser_factor=Inf — keep every confuser patch.\nsupersample=1: anti-aliasing supersample factor forwarded to canonicalize for every patch view and scale. At the large scales (64/96/128) one output texel spans many source pixels, so the default single-tap sampling aliases; >1 samples on a finer grid and box-averages down. Cost scales as supersample² and is paid at every scale, including the small ones that do not alias. 3–4 is the quality sweet spot.\np_downsample=0.75: probability of the resolution-loss augmentation firing on a frame (down- then up-sample to simulate blur/scale loss; conditional on firing, the three tiers 0.5×/0.25×/0.125× are equally likely). Independent of the perspective warp — both can fire on one frame. 0 disables it.\np_perspective=0.5: probability of applying the perspective augmentation to a frame. It warps the frame with a homography that strengthens the primary (highest-quality) board's existing foreshortening, keeping the board centroid fixed, and composes the same warp into every board's homography. Independent of the resolution-loss augmentation — both can fire on one frame. 0 disables it.\nperspective_strengthen_max=1.0: upper bound of the strengthening factor. The board plane's vanishing line is scaled by 1+γ with γ drawn uniformly from [0, perspective_strengthen_max], so 0 reproduces the frame's real perspective and larger values exaggerate it along the board's own tilt.\nperspective_jitter_max=0.1: upper bound of the extra random-direction tilt added on top of the strengthening, in a board-normalized frame (world extent → [-1,1]²), so the magnitude is unit- and board-size-invariant.\nblur_sigma_max=0: upper bound of the Gaussian blur augmentation. When positive, every frame is blurred with σ ~ U[0, blur_sigma_max] (px) before extraction; 0 disables it. Unlike the other two augmentations this one is unconditional once enabled — there is no separate probability — so it shifts the whole training distribution rather than sampling from it. Independent of the resolution-loss and perspective augmentations.\nwrite_anchors=true: write one per-board GT anchor sequence (named by uid) before processing media. Set false to skip them — useful for false-board diagnostics that only need the <media>_<uid> tracked sequences, since the anchor galleries are large and slow to compress.\nwriter_buffer=1: depth of the async-writer queue. A single background task owns the output file and does all HDF5 compression/writes while the GPU thread builds the next sequence (overlap needs julia -t ≥2). Larger values give more slack but hold more finished (large) sequences in memory; 0 = rendezvous.\nasync_writer=true: overlap HDF5 compression with GPU compute via the writer task above. Set false to write synchronously on the GPU thread (no overlap) — for -t 1 / memory-constrained runs.\noutput_dir=\".\", output_name=\"tracks\": output $output_name.tracks location\nbackend=default_device(): compute backend for adapt/canonicalize/describe (CUDABackend() when CUDA is loaded, otherwise KA.CPU())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.build_tracks_for_sequences"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.build_absolute_tracks","page":"Public API","text":"build_absolute_tracks(boards, model, video_path; board_feature_dict,\n                      board_bundles, kwargs...) -> output_path\n\nBuild multi-modal tracks for one video against a set of boards (see build_tracks_for_sequences for the higher-level driver).\n\nboards is a Dict{uid => board} (each a load_board NamedTuple with an extra image field), board_feature_dict a Dict{uid => features} and board_bundles a Dict{uid => BoardBundle} — all keyed by the same uid that track_boards returns. One .tracks sequence is written per board that gets tracked, named $(video)_$(uid).\n\nSampling, filtering, patch and augmentation keywords (n_frames/stride, iou_threshold, max_detections, max_image_pixels, patch_type, scale, confuser_factor, supersample, p_downsample, p_perspective, perspective_strengthen_max, perspective_jitter_max, blur_sigma_max) behave as documented on build_tracks_for_sequences, which forwards them here. patch_size=64 is local to this function. output_name is used verbatim (the higher-level driver adds the _<patch_type>_s<scale> suffix). sink receives each finished sequence as (name, tracks, confusers, is_anchor); the default writes it synchronously. on_view_stats, when given, receives this media item's viewing-angle records once, as on_view_stats(stats::Vector{ViewAngleStat}) (sorted by (uid, frame_idx)) — the driver collects them into its run-wide chart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.build_absolute_tracks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.save_tracks","page":"Public API","text":"save_tracks(path, tracks; sequence_name, untracked=nothing, patch_type=nothing,\n            scale=nothing, is_anchor=false) -> path\n\nSave tracks (and optionally untracked/confuser patches) to a .tracks file (HDF5 format). Each media item is stored as a separate sequence group so new items can be appended incrementally to the same file.\n\nHDF5 layout\n\nDict (single-mode — one patch type at one scale, written by build_absolute_tracks / save_board_anchors):\n\n/sequences/<name> — group attribute is_anchor (UInt8, 1 for the per-board GT anchor sequences, 0 for the per-media tracked sequences)\n/sequences/<name>/tracks/patches — (P, P, N) float32, chunked+compressed\n/sequences/<name>/tracks/masks — (P, P, N) float32; per-pixel board∩in-frame mask (1 where the patch pixel lands on the board sheet AND on a real image pixel; 0 over off-board background, image edges and perspective-warp voids)\n/sequences/<name>/tracks/affine_shapes — (2, 2, N) float32\n/sequences/<name>/tracks/frame_id — (N,) int32; source frame of each observation. Tracked sequences only (GT-anchor sequences omit it).\n/sequences/<name>/tracks/homographies — (3, 3, F) float64; ONE world→image homography H_wi per frame the board was detected in (F frames, not N observations). Tracked sequences only.\n/sequences/<name>/tracks/view_angles — (F,) float64; per-frame viewing obliquity in radians (0 = fronto-parallel, → π/2 edge-on), from the local affine tilt of H_wi at the board centroid (intrinsics-free; metric because the board is square). Aligned with homographies.\n/sequences/<name>/tracks/homography_frame_ids — (F,) int32; the frame index of each homographies/view_angles column. Join an observation to its pose via frame_id → this table.\n/sequences/<name>/tracks/track_id, n_tracks, track_lengths\n/sequences/<name>/tracks — group attributes patch_type (string) and scale (float64)\n/sequences/<name>/untracked/patches, untracked/masks — (P, P, M) float32 (present only when confusers were supplied)\n\nA second, Vector{Track} layout used to live here for build_tracks; both it and its reader went with that pipeline (2026-08-19).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.save_tracks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.plot_tracks","page":"Public API","text":"Render one frame of a TrackTrace (detections, predictions, associations). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.plot_tracks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.load","page":"Public API","text":"load(path::AbstractString) -> (bundle, image)\n\nLoad a blob board or pattern from disk, including both the JSON metadata and image.\n\nAccepts any of the paired files (image or JSON) and automatically finds the sidecar:\n\n.png → finds .json sidecar\n.pdf → finds .json sidecar (renders at correct DPI from metadata)\n.json → finds image (.png or .pdf)\n\nReturns a tuple (bundle, image) where:\n\nbundle: Either BoardBundle or PatternBundle (automatically detected from JSON)\nimage: Loaded image array\n\nSee also: load_bundle for metadata-only loading (no rasterization).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.load"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.save","page":"Public API","text":"save(pattern::AbstractMatrix, bundle::PatternBundle; dir, format=\"png\", overwrite=false, pretty_json=true)\n\nSave a grayscale pattern image directly and its JSON sidecar. Saves the colorant array directly as a grayscale PNG (single channel, no alpha). Returns (img_path, json_path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.save"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.canonical_config_json","page":"Public API","text":"canonical_config_json(config) -> String\n\nThe blobboards canonical (v1) encoding of a config's lowered tree — the byte string board identities are minted from. Stable across JSON3/serializer versions by construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.canonical_config_json"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.walk_media","page":"Public API","text":"walk_media(root; videos = true, images = true) -> Vector{String}\n\nEvery image and video under root, recursively, sorted for reproducible order.\n\nroot may also be a single file (returned as a one-element vector if it is media) so callers need not special-case it. Supply videos = false or images = false to restrict the kind — a still corpus belongs in find_boards and a video corpus in track_boards, so they are usually walked separately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.walk_media"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.media_resolution","page":"Public API","text":"media_resolution(path) -> Size2\n\nFrame size of the image or video at path, as a Size2 (width, height).\n\nVideos report it from stream metadata without decoding; images fall back to a decode, since Julia's image stack exposes no general header-only probe. Costs ~0.6 s for a video and ~0.8 s for a 24 MP still, so it is a preliminary probe, not something to call per frame.\n\nSize2 rather than a tuple ON PURPOSE: VideoIO.out_frame_size is (width, height) while Base.size is (rows, cols), and a bare tuple cannot say which it holds — that ambiguity produced a real transposition bug here. The named fields make both branches state the same thing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.media_resolution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.group_by_resolution","page":"Public API","text":"group_by_resolution(paths; probe = media_resolution)\n    -> Vector{Pair{Size2, Vector{String}}}\n\nGroup media by frame resolution, largest group first, order preserved within each group.\n\nProcess each group together and call VisualGeometryFeatures.clear_asift_plans!() at the boundary: one plan build per group instead of one per resolution change. Also stage the gallery once (GalleryIndex) outside the loop and supply it as gpu_index — it is independent of resolution, so rebuilding it per medium re-uploads the whole descriptor set for nothing.\n\nORDER IS SAFE AT MEDIA GRANULARITY ONLY. Never reorder frames within one medium: track_boards predicts each frame from the previous two, so shuffling a sequence destroys the temporal association. Every frame of a video shares one resolution anyway, so a video is intrinsically one group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.group_by_resolution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.is_video","page":"Public API","text":"is_video(path) -> Bool\n\nWhether path names a video source (by extension) — the classification walk_media and the annotation drivers share.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.is_video"},{"category":"function","location":"api/public/#BlobBoards.example_data","page":"Public API","text":"example_data(name::Symbol) -> String\n\nReturn the directory of one of the package's example-data artifacts, fetched on first use.\n\nname is :boards (50 board-definition JSONs), :still (one calibrated 4240x2832 image + cameras.json) or :video (one calibrated 1080p clip + cameras.json). Each is fetched from the Hugging Face examples dataset through the package catalog (Artifacts.toml) into the shared artifact store. The pipeline examples run entirely on these: a fresh ] add BlobBoards needs no dataset, no data_dir, no configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.example_data"},{"category":"section","location":"api/patterns/#Pattern-API","page":"Patterns","text":"","title":"Pattern API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/patterns/#Main-Functions","page":"Patterns","text":"See Public API for blob_pattern.","title":"Main Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/patterns/#Internal-Functions","page":"Patterns","text":"","title":"Internal Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/patterns/#Blob-Placement","page":"Patterns","text":"","title":"Blob Placement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/patterns/#Scale-Calculation","page":"Patterns","text":"The following internal functions handle scale distribution:\n\ncalc_blob_scales(min_scale, max_scale; scales_per_octave = 3) - The scale ladder from the smallest to the largest σ (px), both exact, evenly spaced in log\ncalc_scale_counts(pattern_size, scales; ...) - Calculate blob counts per scale","title":"Scale Calculation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/patterns/#Rendering","page":"Patterns","text":"render_blob!(pattern, blob; extent) - Render a single Gaussian blob to the pattern image","title":"Rendering"},{"category":"function","location":"api/patterns/#BlobBoards.place_blobs","page":"Patterns","text":"place_blobs(rng::MersenneTwister, pattern_size::Size2{<:VgcQuantity}, blob_counts; kwargs...) -> Vector{Disc}\n\nPlace isotropic blobs without overlap using an R-tree for fast collision checks. Larger scales are placed first to improve packing. Returns a vector of placed blobs.\n\nInternally uses an R-tree with index-only storage (like NMS) for efficient spatial queries.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nα=1.2: sampling bias parameter\nmax_trials=20000: per-blob insertion trial cap\ncutoff: Gaussian truncation radius multiplier (in units of σ)\nraster_aligned=false: align blob centers to raster grid. This low-level default is NOT what patterns use: blob_pattern, PatternConfig and blob_board default to true (2026-07-28) and always supply it explicitly (as they supply max_trials, default 5000 there).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.place_blobs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Types","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Configuration-Types","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Configuration Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Bundle-Types","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Bundle Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Layout-Types","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Layout Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Ruler-Types","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Ruler Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Constants","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Constants"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#UID-Generation","page":"Types","text":"","title":"UID Generation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.PatternConfig","page":"Types","text":"PatternConfig\n\nDeterministic configuration for pattern generation.\n\nFields (selected)\n\npattern_resolution::Size2: logical size of the pattern region\nmin_scale: minimum σ (in logical units)\nmax_scale: maximum σ (in logical units) — the top of the scale ladder, exact (see calc_blob_scales). The constructor keyword's nothing (its default) stores a disc diameter of 20% of the short side; the field is always a σ. Users give sizes through blob_sizes (printed disc diameters) on blob_pattern/blob_board; this struct stores only σ in px (schema v2).\nseed::UInt64: RNG seed for reproducibility\npixel_type: requested output colorant type (e.g. Gray{N0f16})\npolarity::Symbol: blob polarity (:dark for dark blobs on light background, :light for light blobs on dark background)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.PatternConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.BoardConfig","page":"Types","text":"BoardConfig\n\nPhysical board configuration including canvas, print density, border, and ruler. Construct using physical units (e.g. mm or inch).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.BoardConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.BoardPreamble","page":"Types","text":"BoardPreamble\n\nMetadata for a board, bundling the physical BoardConfig and the embedded PatternConfig used to render the pattern region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.BoardPreamble"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.PatternBundle","page":"Types","text":"PatternBundle <: AbstractBundle\n\nContainer returned by blob_pattern with configuration and blob list.\n\nFields:\n\npattern_config::PatternConfig\nblobs::Vector{Disc}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.PatternBundle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.BoardBundle","page":"Types","text":"BoardBundle <: AbstractBundle\n\nContainer returned by blob_board with preamble, generated blobs, and optional saddle points.\n\nFields:\n\npreamble::BoardPreamble\nblobs::Vector{Disc} - blob locations in logical units (pixels, COLMAP convention)\nsaddle_points::Vector{Point2{VgcQuantity{Float64}}} - saddle point locations in pixel units (only for frame_style=:saddle)\n\nBlobs and saddle points are stored internally in logical (pixel) coordinates in :pattern reference frame. Use blobs(bundle, reference_frame; units=mm) or saddle_points(bundle, reference_frame; units=mm) to extract in different frames or units.\n\nnote: `saddle_points` is EMPTY on every board in circulation\nIt is populated only by frame_style = :saddle, and no printed board uses it: all 50 boards in the shipped artifact are frame_style = :ruler with zero saddle points (checked 2026-09-10). The detection pipeline never reads this field either — correspondences are built from blob CENTROIDS alone, so a board's saddles play no part in detection, matching or pose.An empty vector here is therefore normal, not a defect or a load failure. The capability itself is live and tested (plot_saddle in the authoring extension, saddle_points_python in the Python one) — it is simply unused by the current board population.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.BoardBundle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.LogicalBoardSizes","page":"Types","text":"LogicalBoardSizes\n\nDerived logical sizes (in dots) for a board layout at a given density. Provides canvas, pattern, corner, and frame sizes used by layout_grid.\n\nThe margin field is a 4-tuple (left, right, bottom, top) of integral padding values suitable for Makie's figure_padding parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.LogicalBoardSizes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.RulerOrientation","page":"Types","text":"RulerOrientation\n\nEnum for specifying ruler orientation on calibration boards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.RulerOrientation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.RulerTick","page":"Types","text":"RulerTick{T}\n\nTick specification for a printed ruler. Fields\n\nspacing::T: center-to-center distance between ticks\nwidth::T: the tick's full drawn length across the frame band (physical)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.RulerTick"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.RulerSpec","page":"Types","text":"RulerSpec{RT<:RulerTick}\n\nRuler specification: the major_tick and minor_tick spacings and widths.\n\nA tick's width is its FULL drawn length across the frame band, in physical units, centred in the band. BoardConfig's defaults (major = the padded band frame_width − 2·frame_padding, minor = half of it) fill the band with major ticks. Requires 0 < minor ≤ major ≤ frame_width − 2·frame_padding (checked by BoardConfig for frame_style = :ruler).\n\nSchema: board bundles before blobboards/board/v3 stored a third field, width (the \"ruler width\"), and HALF-extent tick widths; the reader drops that field and doubles the tick widths (StructTypes.construct below), which reproduces exactly the ruler those boards were drawn with. The stored hashes.config stays their identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.RulerSpec"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.PRINT_SIZES","page":"Types","text":"PRINT_SIZES\n\nMapping of common paper sizes (ISO/ANSI names) to physical Size2 dimensions. Use with blob_board(paper_size::String, ...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.PRINT_SIZES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/types/#BlobBoards.hash_config","page":"Types","text":"hash_config(config)\n\nHash a configuration (PatternConfig or BoardPreamble) — SHA-256 over the canonical v1 encoding (canonical_config_json); full hex string.\n\nMinting only: identities of existing boards come from their STORED hashes.config (authoritative on load), never from recomputation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.hash_config"},{"category":"section","location":"api/io/#I/O-API","page":"I/O","text":"","title":"I/O API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/io/#Loading-and-Saving","page":"I/O","text":"See Public API for load and save.","title":"Loading and Saving"},{"category":"section","location":"api/io/#Internal-Functions","page":"I/O","text":"","title":"Internal Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/io/#File-Naming","page":"I/O","text":"","title":"File Naming"},{"category":"function","location":"api/io/#BlobBoards.make_filebase","page":"I/O","text":"make_filebase(bundle::AbstractBundle; config_hash = hash_config(…)) -> String\n\nBase filename (without extension) computed from the bundle type and configuration hash. Returns \"blobboard<hash>\" for BoardBundle and \"blobpattern<hash>\" for PatternBundle, where <hash> is the first 8 characters of the hex-encoded SHA-256 hash of the complete configuration, formatted as XXX_XXXXX (3 chars underscore 5 chars) for readability.\n\nFor BoardBundle: hash is SHA-256 of preamble (PatternConfig + BoardConfig) For PatternBundle: hash is SHA-256 of PatternConfig. A bundle loaded from disk must be given its stored config_hash (hashes.config), its identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlobBoards.make_filebase"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Pattern-Generation","page":"Patterns","text":"This guide covers the details of blob pattern generation.","title":"Pattern Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Pattern-Basics","page":"Patterns","text":"A blob pattern is a collection of Gaussian blobs placed on a white background without overlap. The blobs are distributed across multiple scales (sizes) following a logarithmic distribution.\n\nusing BlobBoards\nusing VisualGeometryCore: px\n\n# Basic pattern generation\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing))","title":"Pattern Basics"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Scale-Distribution","page":"Patterns","text":"blob_sizes = (min, max) sets the range of blob sizes, as the diameter of the rendered disc (2·cutoff·σ):\n\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600;\n    blob_sizes = (12px, nothing), # Smallest disc 12 px across; default max\n    scales_per_octave = 3,        # Rungs per doubling of size\n)\n\n# Discs from 12 px to 120 px across\nblob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes = (12px, 120px))\n\n# A max as a fraction of the pattern's short side\nblob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes = (12px, 0.2short_side))\n\nFor blob_pattern the sizes are logical (px; a bare number is px). For blob_board they are physical (mm, inch), converted through the print density. Either way they are stored as σ in px (config.min_scale, config.max_scale). The default max (nothing) is a disc 20% of the short side across; the default min, 2·cutoff·2px (8 px at the default cutoff), is also the floor (σ ≥ 2 px).","title":"Scale Distribution"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Understanding-Scales","page":"Patterns","text":"A blob is a Gaussian of standard deviation σ, rendered as a disc of radius cutoff · σ; blob_sizes give that disc's diameter, 2 · cutoff · σ, and the config stores σ.\n\ncutoff is the Gaussian truncation radius in units of σ. Use cutoff = 2: the default (DEFAULT_CUTOFF), the cutoff the shipped boards were printed with, and the radius the detector's NMS and the adaptation default to. Leave it unset rather than spelling another value; a wider cutoff only spends board area on the Gaussian's near-white tail.","title":"Understanding Scales"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#The-Scale-Ladder","page":"Patterns","text":"The blob scales form a geometric ladder from the min to the max, both exact:\n\nk  = round(scales_per_octave · log2(max / min))      # number of steps\nσᵢ = min · (max / min)^(i / k),   i = 0 … k\n\nso the steps are evenly spaced in log at about 1/scales_per_octave octave. When the max lies exactly on the 2^(1/scales_per_octave) ladder the steps are exactly min · 2^(i / scales_per_octave) (every v1 board is such a case). When max = nothing, the default caps the largest disc DIAMETER at 20% of the pattern's short side, i.e. a σ of 0.2 · min(width, height) / (2 · cutoff). The ladder itself is calc_blob_scales(min_scale, max_scale; scales_per_octave = 3) (unitless px).","title":"The Scale Ladder"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Placement-Algorithm","page":"Patterns","text":"Blobs are placed using rejection sampling with R-tree collision detection:\n\nLarger scales are placed first (greedy packing)\nEach blob attempts random placement up to max_trials times\nCollision is checked using cutoff radius\nIf raster_aligned=true, centers snap to pixel grid\n\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600;\n    blob_sizes = (12px, nothing),\n    max_trials = 10000,       # Attempts per blob\n    α = 1.2,                  # Scale sampling bias (higher = more small blobs)\n    cutoff = 2.0,             # Gaussian truncation radius in σ (default; recommended)\n    raster_aligned = true,    # Snap centers to pixel centers (default)\n)","title":"Placement Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Rendering","page":"Patterns","text":"Blobs are rendered directly at target resolution: the per-blob normalization pins the truncated Gaussian's boundary to background level (a continuous profile), and with raster_aligned = true (the default) every center sits on a pixel center, so point sampling is symmetric per blob — no oversampling step is needed or supported.","title":"Rendering"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Pixel-Types","page":"Patterns","text":"Control the output pixel format with the leading type argument — the pixel type is positional, as types are in Julia; pixel_type = … as a keyword is an ArgumentError (a PatternConfig still records it as a field):\n\nusing Colors: Gray\nusing FixedPointNumbers: N0f8, N0f16\n\n# 8-bit grayscale (default, matches PDF limitations)\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(Gray{N0f8}, Size2(width=800, height=600);\n    blob_sizes = (12px, nothing)\n)\n\n# 16-bit grayscale (higher precision)\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(Gray{N0f16}, Size2(width=800, height=600);\n    blob_sizes = (12px, nothing)\n)","title":"Pixel Types"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Reproducibility","page":"Patterns","text":"Patterns are fully deterministic when seeded:\n\n# Same seed = identical pattern\np1, b1, _ = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), seed=0x1234)\np2, b2, _ = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), seed=0x1234)\n# p1 == p2 and b1 == b2\n\n# From saved configuration\nbundle, image = load(\"blob_pattern.json\")\nconfig = pattern_config(bundle)\n# Regenerate identical pattern from config","title":"Reproducibility"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Blob-Polarity","page":"Patterns","text":"By default, blobs are dark on a light background. Use the polarity parameter for light blobs on dark:\n\n# Dark blobs on white background (default)\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), polarity=:dark)\n\n# Light blobs on dark background\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), polarity=:light)","title":"Blob Polarity"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Blob-Coordinates","page":"Patterns","text":"Blobs are stored in COLMAP convention internally (first pixel center at 0.5, 0.5):\n\nbundle, _ = load(\"blob_pattern.json\")\n\n# Get blobs in COLMAP convention\nb = blobs(bundle)\n\n# Convert to other conventions using Translation (extends CoordinateTransformations,\n# is unit-bearing, composes via ∘).\nb_opencv = Translation(:colmap, :opencv).(b)\nb_julia = Translation(:colmap, :julia).(b)","title":"Blob Coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Configuration-Object","page":"Patterns","text":"The PatternConfig captures all generation parameters:\n\nconfig = PatternConfig(\n    pattern_resolution = Size2(width=800px, height=600px),\n    min_scale = 3.0px,          # stored σ, px\n    max_scale = nothing,        # stored σ, px; nothing = default rule\n    seed = 0x1234,\n    pixel_type = Gray{N0f8},\n    scales_per_octave = 3,\n    max_trials = 5000,\n    α = 1.2,\n    cutoff = 2.0,\n    raster_aligned = true,\n    polarity = :dark,\n)\n\npattern, blobs = blob_pattern(config)","title":"Configuration Object"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Saving-Patterns","page":"Patterns","text":"# Save during generation\npattern, blobs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), dir=\"./output\")\n\n# Or save explicitly\nbundle = PatternBundle(config, blobs)\nsave(pattern, bundle; dir=\"./output\")\n\nOutput files:\n\nblob_pattern_XXX_XXXXX.png - Grayscale pattern image\nblob_pattern_XXX_XXXXX.json - Full configuration and blob list","title":"Saving Patterns"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Overview","page":"Overview","text":"Generate deterministic multi-scale blob patterns and printable calibration boards for computer vision.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Overview-2","page":"Overview","text":"BlobBoards.jl creates calibration targets with blob patterns that are:\n\nDeterministic: Bit-exact reproducible via seeded configuration\nMulti-scale: Logarithmically distributed blob sizes for scale-invariant detection\nPrint-ready: PDF output with rulers, QR codes, and metadata\nConfigurable: Physical units (mm, inch) with compile-time unit checking","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Quick-Start","page":"Overview","text":"using BlobBoards      # re-exports the units: mm, inch, px, dpi, …\nusing CairoMakie      # loads the board-authoring extension (blob_board)\n\n# Generate a printable A4 calibration board\nbundle = blob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; blob_sizes=(4.0mm, nothing), seed=0x1234, dir=\"./output\")\n\n# Generate a pattern for training/simulation (core package only)\npattern, discs, config = blob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes=(12px, nothing), seed=0x1234)\n\n# Extract blobs from saved files\nbundle, _ = BlobBoards.load(\"blob_board.json\")\nb = blobs(bundle, :pattern; units=mm)\n\nCairoMakie is a weak dependency, so it cannot be loaded from the package's own --project=. environment; use the checkout's authoring/ companion env (julia --project=authoring) or add CairoMakie to your own environment. See Getting Started. Without it blob_board has no methods.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Key-Concepts","page":"Overview","text":"","title":"Key Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Patterns-vs-Boards","page":"Overview","text":"Pattern (blob_pattern): A blob pattern image in pixel/logical units. Used for training, simulation, or embedding in custom layouts.\nBoard (blob_board): A complete calibration board with borders, rulers, QR code, and UID markers. Ready for printing.","title":"Patterns vs Boards"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Unit-System","page":"Overview","text":"Units are enforced at compile-time via DynamicQuantities.jl, through VisualGeometryCore's VgcQuantity. BlobBoards re-exports mm, cm, inch, px, dpi, and friends, so using BlobBoards is all you need:\n\nUnit Type Examples Description\nLogical px Pixels (dimensionless)\nPhysical mm, inch, cm Real-world lengths\nDensity dpi Dots per inch\n\nCritical distinction:\n\nblob_pattern(): blob_sizes are disc diameters in logical units (px, e.g. (12px, nothing))\nblob_board(): blob_sizes are printed disc diameters in physical units (mm, inch, e.g. (4.0mm, nothing))\nBoth are stored as σ in px (min_scale/max_scale; a disc's diameter is 2·cutoff·σ)","title":"Unit System"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Coordinate-Specifications","page":"Overview","text":"BlobBoards uses three types of coordinate specifications:\n\nType Values Purpose\nImage Origin :colmap, :opencv, :julia Pixel coordinate convention\nReference Frame :pattern, :board, :canvas Spatial origin on boards\nCoordinate System :logical, :physical, :world Unit system for transforms\n\nSee the Coordinates Guide for detailed documentation.","title":"Coordinate Specifications"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Features","page":"Overview","text":"Authoring\n\nDeterministic patterns from seeded configurations\nPhysical units via DynamicQuantities (mm, inch, dpi, etc.)\nPrintable boards (PDF) or training images (PNG) with ruler, saddle, or blank frames\nJSON serialization for exact reproduction\nBlob extraction with multiple coordinate conventions\nWorld coordinates for 3D calibration workflows\n\nDetection and pose\n\nfind_boards — detect blobs, fit elliptical frames, canonicalize, describe, match against a board gallery, and estimate pose (P3P or homography)\nMany boards located per image, each accepted on spatial consistency rather than quad segmentation, so partial occlusion is survivable\nBackend-generic hot path (KernelAbstractions): CPU, CUDA, or any KA backend\ntrack_boards for temporally warm-started video tracking\n\nTooling\n\nTraining pipeline: detect, track, and extract canonicalized patches from video\nFileIO integration for .tracks files (HDF5 format)\nVisualization helpers for Makie backends","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Installation","page":"Overview","text":"With pixi (recommended):\n\ngit clone https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/BlobBoards.jl\ncd BlobBoards.jl\npixi install\npixi run julia --project=.\n\nManual setup: Requires Julia 1.12+ and Ghostscript (for PDF compression). See the Getting Started guide for detailed instructions, including the CairoMakie environment that board authoring needs.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Contents","page":"Overview","text":"Pages = [\n    \"guide/reference.md\",\n    \"guide/getting-started.md\",\n    \"guide/patterns.md\",\n    \"guide/boards.md\",\n    \"guide/coordinates.md\",\n    \"guide/board-tracks.md\",\n    \"guide/python.md\",\n    \"api/public.md\",\n    \"api/types.md\",\n    \"api/patterns.md\",\n    \"api/boards.md\",\n    \"api/io.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Index","page":"Overview","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Role-in-the-VisualGeometryToolkit-stack","page":"Overview","text":"BlobBoards provides fiducial blob-board targets for the VisualGeometryToolkit stack: deterministic pattern generation, printable boards, detection, and calibration-grade correspondence ground truth. It consumes VisualGeometryCore.jl (geometry, units, blob detection) and RobustVisualGeometry.jl (robust correspondence/pose estimation).\n\nAll toolkit documentation is aggregated at the portal: https://visualgeometrytoolkit.github.io.","title":"Role in the VisualGeometryToolkit stack"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"<div id=\"landing\" class=\"landing\">\n<header class=\"landing-hero\">\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__text\">\n    <p class=\"landing-name\" style=\"--landing-name-chars: 10\">BlobBoards</p>\n    <h1 class=\"landing-title\">Robust Markers for Accurate Pose</h1>\n    <p class=\"landing-tagline\">James Pritts, Till Sittart, Hendrik Sauer, Silja Janßen, Felix Seegräber, David Nakath, Kevin Köser\nMarine Data Science\nKiel University\nBMVC 2026</p>\n    <div class=\"landing-actions\">\n<a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--brand\" href=\"guide/getting-started/\">Getting started</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.28830\">Paper</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"api/public/\">API Reference</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/BlobBoards.jl\">Code</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__image\">\n    <div class=\"landing-hero__image-container\">\n      <img class=\"docs-light-only\" src=\"assets/landing/hero_board.png\" alt=\"A square BlobBoard, a multi-scale field of Gaussian blobs inside a ruler frame, with uid glyphs in three corners and a QR code in the fourth.\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n      <img class=\"docs-dark-only\" src=\"assets/landing/hero_board_dark.png\" alt=\"A square BlobBoard, a multi-scale field of Gaussian blobs inside a ruler frame, with uid glyphs in three corners and a QR code in the fourth.\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</header>\n<section class=\"landing-features\" aria-label=\"Features\">\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"guide/boards/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🖨️</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Author a board</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>blob_board</code> renders a printable PDF board in physical units (<code>mm</code>, <code>dpi</code>) from a seed, with rulers to check the print scale and a JSON sidecar that reproduces it.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"guide/detection/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🎯</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Detect boards and estimate pose</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>find_boards</code> finds every board in an image, identifies it against your gallery, and returns its pose — verified by hundreds of blob correspondences.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"guide/python/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🐍</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Use it from Python</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">The <code>blobboards</code> package gives the same board authoring and detection in Python, returning NumPy arrays and dataclasses.</p>\n  </a>\n</section>\n</div>\n\nA dense, multi-scale field of Gaussian blobs and a feature-based pipeline for joint detection, identification, and pose estimation.\n\n(Image: All 25 BlobBoards in a cluttered scene, detected, identified and registered)\n\nAll 25 BlobBoards in the image are detected, identified, and registered against a 50-board gallery — 25 of its entries are absent confusers, and identification is still perfect. Colour distinguishes detections; the overlaid pattern shows registration accuracy across large variations in scale, distance, and obliquity.","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Abstract","page":"Home","text":"We propose BlobBoards, a fiducial marker system comprising a dense, multi-scale field of Gaussian blobs and a feature-based pipeline for joint detection, identification, and pose estimation. Each board is registered from hundreds of blob features whose dense spatial coverage constrains pose, while multiple scales preserve detectability across large changes in focal length, distance, and obliquity. Learned local descriptors are matched to the reference pattern and spatially verified, so the correspondences determine pose and certify identity. Against motion-capture ground truth, BlobBoards achieve median translation errors of 3.6–5.0 mm, reducing AprilTag's median translation error by 89% on small boards and 70% on large ones. They also produce far fewer large-rotation failures than state-of-the-art tag systems. BlobBoards achieve the highest detection rate, 80% versus 74% for AprilTag and 58% for ArUco, with the largest margin on the smallest markers. Under 50% occlusion, they still detect 69% of boards with essentially unchanged median translation error, while AprilTag and ArUco detect none.","title":"Abstract"},{"category":"section","location":"#Install","page":"Home","text":"Copy the command for your language and install method.","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"#Julia","page":"Home","text":"pixi — installs Julia, Python and Ghostscript and resolves the package environment; then using BlobBoards:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/BlobBoards.jl && cd BlobBoards.jl && pixi install && pixi run julia --project=.\n\nPkg — from the General registry (Julia 1.12+; Ghostscript ≥ 10.07 for the PDF print master):\n\nusing Pkg; Pkg.add(\"BlobBoards\")","title":"Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"#Python","page":"Home","text":"pixi — installs the blobboards Python package together with the Julia core; the example writes an A4 board to ./output:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/BlobBoards.jl && cd BlobBoards.jl && pixi install && pixi run python python/examples/boards/generate_a4_board.py\n\npip — installs blobboards with its juliacall and numpy dependencies into your current Python environment:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/BlobBoards.jl && cd BlobBoards.jl/python && pip install -e .","title":"Python"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-started","page":"Home","text":"Install BlobBoards and generate, print and detect your first board in Getting Started. Authoring boards covers the authoring environment, every board option and printing; Detecting boards covers find_boards, pose estimation and GPU acceleration. The Overview introduces patterns, boards and the coordinate conventions.\n\nusing BlobBoards, CairoMakie   # CairoMakie loads the board-authoring extension\n\n# A4 board at 300 dpi, smallest printed disc 4 mm across → board PDF + JSON in ./out\nblob_board(\"A4\", 300dpi; blob_sizes = (4.0mm, nothing), seed = 0xBEEF, dir = \"./out\")\n\n# Or a pixel pattern for simulation and training\nblob_pattern(800, 600; blob_sizes = (12px, nothing), seed = 0x1234, dir = \"./out\")","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"#Method","page":"Home","text":"A board is registered from hundreds of blob features, not from four corners — that is where the accuracy and the occlusion robustness come from.\n\n(a) (b) (c) (d)\n(Image: Affine blob detections) (Image: Detections adapted to anisotropic ellipses) (Image: Inliers as 64×64 log-polar patches) (Image: Reference pattern projected under the recovered pose)\n\n(a) Detect: affine detections from the ASIFT scale space. (b) Adapt: IRLS adaptation to anisotropic ellipses; the twelve pose inliers are coloured individually. (c) Canonicalize: those inliers mapped to 64×64 log-polar patches. (d) Match & verify: descriptors matched and spatially verified; the pattern projected under the recovered pose, with reference blob centres in green at opacity proportional to their geometric consistency.\n\nGenerate. A seeded pattern packs blobs from several octaves of scale into one field. The same seed reproduces the same board, in Julia or Python.\nAdapt and register. Each detected blob gets a fitted elliptical frame, is canonicalized into a patch, and described by a learned log-polar network. Matches to the reference pattern are spatially verified, so the correspondences determine pose and certify identity.\nEvaluate. Poses are scored against motion-capture ground truth on a rig of boards at a range of sizes, distances, and angles, alongside AprilTag and ArUco.","title":"Method"},{"category":"section","location":"#Results","page":"Home","text":"Against motion-capture ground truth, compared with AprilTag and ArUco on the same rig:\n\n \n80% boards detected — AprilTag 74%, ArUco 58% — with the largest margin on the smallest markers\n3.6–5.0 mm median translation error, small to large boards\n−89% AprilTag's median translation error on small boards (−70% on large ones)\n69% boards still detected at 50% occlusion, with essentially unchanged median translation error; AprilTag and ArUco detect none","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"#Data-and-models","page":"Home","text":"The paper's data is frozen on Zenodo; the networks and example data the package ships live on Hugging Face.\n\nReproducing the BMVC results. Everything the paper's numbers were computed from is archived at doi:10.5281/zenodo.21998311: the absolute-pose captures with camera calibrations, motion-capture ground truth and hand-eye calibrations; the AprilTag and ArUco baseline detections; the 0–90% occlusion series; the frozen descriptor training set; and the network the reported poses were produced with. It is packaged as sha256-pinned Julia artifact tarballs that bmvc2026/Artifacts.toml fetches lazily.\nProduction networks. blobboards-descriptor holds the ONNX descriptor networks; the package's Artifacts.toml is an add-only, commit-pinned catalog of them. blobboards-examples holds the boards, still image and video the bundled examples run on.","title":"Data & models"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"BlobBoards is described in BlobBoards: Robust Markers for Accurate Pose, BMVC 2026 (PDF, with supplementary material). If you use this project for your research, please cite:\n\n@inproceedings{pritts2026blobboards,\n  author    = {Pritts, James and Sittart, Till and Sauer, Hendrik and\n               Jan{\\ss}en, Silja and Seegr{\\\"a}ber, Felix and\n               Nakath, David and K{\\\"o}ser, Kevin},\n  title     = {{BlobBoards}: Robust Markers for Accurate Pose},\n  booktitle = {British Machine Vision Conference (BMVC)},\n  year      = {2026},\n  eprint    = {2608.28830},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n}\n\nThe default homography path needs no additional citation. The opt-in calibrated P3P path of find_boards (PoseGeometry) runs the published solver of Ding et al. (CVPR 2023); if you use it, please also cite:\n\n@inproceedings{ding2023p3p,\n  author    = {Ding, Yaqing and Yang, Jian and Larsson, Viktor and\n               Olsson, Carl and {\\AA}str{\\\"o}m, Kalle},\n  title     = {Revisiting the {P3P} Problem},\n  booktitle = {IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  pages     = {4872--4880},\n  year      = {2023},\n}","title":"Citation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Support","page":"Home","text":"Please use the GitHub issue tracker for bug reports, questions, and feature requests. The repository's examples/ directory has runnable Julia scripts for generation, detection, tracking and building training tracks; python/examples/ covers the Python package.","title":"Support"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgments","page":"Home","text":"James Pritts is funded by Kiel Training for Excellence, an EU Horizon Europe programme under the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA), grant agreement No. 101081480. The motion-capture setup was funded through the project OP der Zukunft as part of the REACT-EU program and provided by the Kurt Semm Centre for laparoscopic and robot-assisted surgery at the University Hospital of Schleswig-Holstein.","title":"Acknowledgments"}]
}
